[moddesc   {Pdf document generation}]
[titledesc {Pdf document generation}]
[require Tcl 8.6]
[require pdf4tcl [opt 0.9.4.52]]
[description]

This package provides a container class for generating [term {pdf}] documents.

[section COORDINATES]

All coordinates and distances can be expressed with or without a unit. See
[sectref UNITS] for valid units.
When the page is configured with [option -orient] set to false, origin is in
the bottom left corner. With [option -orient] true (the default), origin is in the top left
corner.
Origin is displaced to account for margins, i.e. if margins are 100,
the user coordinate (0,0) corresponds to (100,100) on the paper.
Page option [option -orient] can also affect the anchor point for things like
images.

[section UNITS]
Any coordinates and distances can be expressed with or without an explicit
unit.  If no unit is given, the default unit for the document is used.
A unit may be one of
[option "mm"] (millimeter),
[option "m"] (millimeter),
[option "cm"] (centimeter),
[option "c"] (centimeter),
[option "p"] (points) or
[option "i"] (inches).

Commands returning coordinates or distances always return a double value
in the document's default unit.

[section {PUBLIC API}]

[subsection {PACKAGE COMMANDS}]

[list_begin definitions]

[call [cmd ::pdf4tcl::new] [arg objectName] [opt [arg "option value"]...]]

This command creates a new pdf4tcl object with an associated Tcl
command whose name is [arg objectName]. This [term object] command is
explained in full detail in the sections [sectref {OBJECT COMMAND}]
and [sectref {OBJECT METHODS}]. The object command will be created
under the current namespace if the [arg objectName] is not fully
qualified, and in the specified namespace otherwise.
If [arg objectName] is %AUTO% a name will generated.
The return value is the newly created object's name.

[para]

The options and their values coming after the name of the object are
used to set the initial configuration of the object.
See [sectref {OBJECT CONFIGURATION}].


[call [cmd ::pdf4tcl::getPaperSize] [arg "paper"]]

This call returns the size of a named paper type, e.g. "a4".
Paper names are case insensitive.
The argument [arg "paper"] may also be a two element list
with values as accepted by [cmd ::pdf4tcl::getPoints].
The return value is a list with width and height in points.


[call [cmd ::pdf4tcl::getPaperSizeList]]

This call returns the list of known paper types.


[call [cmd ::pdf4tcl::getPoints] [arg "val"]]

This call translates a measurement to points (1/72 inch).
The format of [arg "val"] is '[arg "num"] [opt [arg "unit"]]' where
[arg "num"] is a valid integer or double. See [sectref UNITS] for valid
[arg "unit"]s.  If no [arg "unit"] is given, the value is interpreted
as points.


[call [cmd ::pdf4tcl::loadBaseTrueTypeFont] [arg "basefontname"] [arg "ttf_file_name"]]

This call loads a TTF font from file to be used by any pdf4tcl objects.
The [arg "basefontname"] is used to reference this font.
To use this base font in documents, a font with some encoding must be
created from it using [cmd createFont], [cmd createFontSpecEnc], or
[cmd createFontSpecCID].

[para]
Only TrueType outline fonts (TTF) are supported. The following font types
are detected and rejected with a descriptive error:

[list_begin itemized]
[item] Color bitmap fonts with CBDT/CBLC tables (e.g. [file NotoColorEmoji.ttf])
[item] Apple bitmap fonts with sbix table (macOS system emoji)
[item] Layered color vector fonts with COLR/CPAL tables (e.g. Segoe UI Emoji on Windows)
[item] OpenType fonts with PostScript outlines (OTTO magic, CFF-based)
[list_end]

[para]
For emoji and symbol coverage, use a vector outline font such as
[file NotoEmoji-Regular.ttf] ([uri "https://fonts.google.com/noto/specimen/Noto+Emoji"])
or [file Symbola.ttf] ([uri "https://dn-works.com/ufas/"]).


[call [cmd ::pdf4tcl::createBaseTrueTypeFont] [arg "basefontname"] [arg "ttf_data"]]

This call creates a base font from TTF binary data.


[call [cmd ::pdf4tcl::loadBaseType1Font] [arg "basefontname"] [arg "AFM_file_name"] [arg "PFB_file_name"]]

This call loads a Type1 font from two files (.afm and .pfb) to be used by
any pdf4tcl objects. The [arg "basefontname"] is used to reference this font.
To use this base font in documents, a font with some encoding must be
created from it using [cmd createFont] or [cmd createFontSpecEnc].


[call [cmd ::pdf4tcl::createBaseType1Font] [arg "basefontname"] [arg "AFM_data"] [arg "PFB_data"]]
 
This call creates a base font from AFM text and PFB binary data.


[call [cmd ::pdf4tcl::createFont] [arg "basefontname"] [arg "fontname"] [arg "encoding_name"]]

This call creates a font that can be used in documents from a base font. The given encoding
defines the (up to) 256 unicode characters that can be drawn when [arg "fontname"] is selected.
To use more characters, multiple fonts need to be created and selected based on what needs to be written.

[comment "String map is used here to be able to include backslash-nl in the example"]
[example [string map {% \\} {
pdf4tcl::loadBaseTrueTypeFont BaseArial "arial.ttf"
pdf4tcl::createFont BaseArial MyArial cp1251 
pdf4tcl::loadBaseType1Font BaseType1 "a010013l.afm" "a010013l.pfb"
pdf4tcl::createFont BaseType1 MyType1 cp1251 
pdf4tcl::new mypdf -paper a4 -compress 0
mypdf startPage
mypdf setFont 10 MyArial
set txt "\u042D\u0442\u043E \u0442\u0435\u043A\u0441\u0442 \u043D\u0430 \u0440\u0443\u0441\u0441\u043A\u043E\u043C%
         \u044F\u0437\u044B\u043A\u0435. This is text in Russian."
mypdf text $txt -bg #CACACA -x 50 -y 100
mypdf setFont 10 MyType1
mypdf text $txt -x 50 -y 200
mypdf write -file fonts.pdf
mypdf destroy
}]]

[call [cmd ::pdf4tcl::createFontSpecEnc] [arg "basefontname"] [arg "fontname"] [arg "subset"]]

This call creates a font that can be used in documents from a base font.
The [arg "subset"] must be a list of (up to 256) unicode values which are the characters that can be
drawn when [arg "fontname"] is selected.

[example {
pdf4tcl::loadBaseTrueTypeFont BaseArial "arial.ttf"
# Subset is a list of unicodes:
for {set f 0} {$f < 128} {incr f} {lappend subset $f}
lappend subset [expr 0xB2] [expr 0x3B2]
pdf4tcl::createFontSpecEnc BaseArial MyArial $subset 
pdf4tcl::new mypdf -paper a4
mypdf startPage
mypdf setFont 16 MyArial
set txt "sin\u00B2\u03B2 + cos\u00B2\u03B2 = 1"
mypdf text $txt -x 50 -y 100
mypdf write -file specenc.pdf
mypdf destroy
}]

[call [cmd ::pdf4tcl::createFontSpecCID] [arg "basefontname"] [arg "fontname"]]

This call creates a Unicode-capable CID font (CIDFontType2 with Identity-H encoding)
from a previously loaded TrueType base font. Unlike [cmd createFont] and [cmd createFontSpecEnc],
which are limited to 256 characters per font instance, a CID font supports any Unicode
character covered by the underlying TTF file. This includes Latin Extended, Greek, Cyrillic,
CJK ideographs, and other scripts. The full TTF binary is embedded in the PDF.

[para]
Characters in the Supplementary Multilingual Plane (SMP, U+10000 and above) are
supported. They are encoded as UTF-16BE surrogate pairs in the ToUnicode CMap,
as required by the PDF specification (ISO 32000, §9.10.3).
This enables correct text extraction and copy-paste from PDF viewers for characters
such as mathematical alphanumerics (U+1D400), musical symbols (U+1D100),
or emoji from vector outline fonts (U+1F300 and above).

[example {
pdf4tcl::loadBaseTrueTypeFont DejaVuSans "/usr/share/fonts/truetype/dejavu/DejaVuSans.ttf"
pdf4tcl::createFontSpecCID DejaVuSans myCIDFont

pdf4tcl::new mypdf -paper a4
mypdf startPage
mypdf setFont 14 myCIDFont
mypdf text "Latin: Hello World" -x 50 -y 750
mypdf text "Extended: \u00e4\u00f6\u00fc\u00df" -x 50 -y 720
mypdf text "Greek: \u0391\u03b2\u03b3\u03b4" -x 50 -y 690
mypdf text "Cyrillic: \u041f\u0440\u0438\u0432\u0435\u0442" -x 50 -y 660
mypdf endPage
mypdf write -file unicode.pdf
mypdf destroy
}]

[call [cmd ::pdf4tcl::getFonts]]

This call returns the list of known font names, i.e. those accepted in a call
to [method setFont].
This includes the default fonts and fonts created by e.g.
[cmd ::pdf4tcl::createFont].

[call [cmd ::pdf4tcl::rgb2Cmyk] [arg "rgb"]]

This call translates an RGB color value to a CMYK color value.
It is used internally if [option -cmyk] was set at object creation to 
translate colors.
You can redefine this procedure to provide your own translation.

[call [cmd ::pdf4tcl::cmyk2Rgb] [arg "cmyk"]]

This call translates a CMYK color value to an RGB color value.
It is used internally to translate colors.
You can redefine this procedure to provide your own translation.

[call [cmd ::pdf4tcl::catPdf] [opt [arg "option value"]...] [arg "infile"] [opt [arg "infile ..."]] [arg "outfile"]]

This call concatenates PDF files into one.
Currently the implementation limits the PDFs a lot since not all details
are taken care of yet. Straightforward ones like those created with pdf4tcl
or ps2pdf should work mostly ok.

Merging keeps the catalog of the FIRST input, and with it its document
information -- two files joined otherwise carry the title of part one. A
merger cannot know what several documents are called together, so it asks:

[list_begin options]
[opt_def -title [arg text]]
[opt_def -author [arg text]]
[opt_def -subject [arg text]]
[opt_def -keywords [arg text]]
[opt_def -creator [arg text]]
[opt_def -producer [arg text]]
[list_end]

An empty value REMOVES the entry -- better no title than the wrong one.
Entries not named keep whatever the first document had.

[example {
::pdf4tcl::catPdf -title "Complete file" -author "" \
        part1.pdf part2.pdf complete.pdf
}]

The options come before the file names, so every existing call keeps
working unchanged.

[para]
Each value is written to BOTH places a PDF carries it: the [const /Info]
dictionary and the XMP packet the catalog points at. ISO 19005-1 clause
6.7.3 requires the two to be equivalent, so a merged PDF/A-1 file with a
new title stays conformant. The packet is edited, not rebuilt -- a
Factur-X or ZUGFeRD extension schema in it survives untouched.

[para]
Note that a proof run against PDF/A-2 or -3 shows nothing here: those
parts dropped clause 6.7.3, and a validator stays silent however far the
two places drift apart. Check against [const 1b].

[para]
Since 0.9.4.49 a file whose cross-reference table is a stream (PDF 1.5+) is
read as well, which covers PDF/A-2 and -3 and therefore ZUGFeRD invoices.
Since 0.9.4.50 objects packed into an object stream ([const /ObjStm]) are
unpacked too, so a file written by [syscmd qpdf] with
[option --object-streams=generate] merges like any other.

[call [cmd ::pdf4tcl::getForms] [arg "infile"]]

This call extracts form data from a PDF file.
The return value is a dictionary with id/info pairs.
The id is the one set with [arg -id] to [method addForm], if the PDF
was generated with pdf4tcl.
The info is a dictionary with the following fields:

[list_begin options]
[opt_def type] Field type.
[opt_def value] Form value.
[opt_def flags] Value of form flags field.
[opt_def default] Default value, if any.
[list_end]

[call [cmd ::pdf4tcl::fillForms] [arg "infile"] [arg "outfile"] [arg "values"]]

Fill the form fields of an existing PDF and write it out again (0.9.4.50+).
Returns the number of fields filled.

[arg values] is a dictionary of field id to value. A text field takes a
string; a check box or radio button takes the state name as it appears in
the file, with the slash ([const /Yes], [const /Off]). [method getForms]
reports the current state under [const default].

A field named in [arg values] but not present in the file raises an error
rather than being ignored: a form that comes out empty with no explanation
is worse than a refused call. Fields present but not named keep their
value.

[example {
pdf4tcl::fillForms empty.pdf filled.pdf {name "Meier" agreed /Yes}
}]

[para]
The value is written, not drawn. [const /NeedAppearances] is set, which
tells the viewer to render it; a viewer that ignores the flag shows the
field as it was, with the value present but invisible. Acrobat and the
common browsers honour it.

[call [cmd ::pdf4tcl::exportForms] [arg "infile"] [arg "outfile"] [opt [arg "options"]]]

Export form field data from a filled PDF as FDF or XFDF (0.9.4.23+).
Returns the number of exported fields.

[list_begin options]
[opt_def -format [arg "fdf|xfdf"]]
Output format. [const fdf] (default): Forms Data Format (ISO 32000 SS12.7.7),
a compact text format supported by most PDF viewers.
[const xfdf]: XML Forms Data Format (ISO 32000 SS12.7.8), human-readable XML.
[opt_def -password [arg "string"]]
Password for encrypted PDFs.
[list_end]

[example {
# Export as FDF
pdf4tcl::exportForms filled.pdf data.fdf

# Export as XFDF
pdf4tcl::exportForms filled.pdf data.xfdf -format xfdf
}]

[list_end]

[subsection {OBJECT COMMAND}]

All commands created by [cmd ::pdf4tcl::new] have the following
general form and may be used to invoke various operations on their
pdf object.

[list_begin definitions]

[call [cmd objectName] [method method] [opt [arg "arg arg ..."]]]

The method [method method] and its [arg arg]'uments determine the exact
behavior of the command. See section [sectref {OBJECT METHODS}] for
the detailed specifications.

[list_end]


[subsection {OBJECT METHODS}]

[list_begin definitions]

[call [arg objectName] [method configure]]

The method returns a list of all known options and their current
values when called without any arguments.


[call [arg objectName] [method configure] [arg option]]

The method behaves like the method [method cget] when called with a
single argument and returns the value of the option specified by said
argument.


[call [arg objectName] [method configure] [option -option] [arg value]...]

The method reconfigures the specified [option option]s of the object,
setting them to the associated [arg value]s, when called with an even
number of arguments, at least two.

[para]

The legal options are described in the section
[sectref {OBJECT CONFIGURATION}].


[call [arg objectName] [method cget] [option -option]]

This method expects a legal configuration option as argument and will
return the current value of that option for the object the method was
invoked for.

[para]

The legal configuration options are described in section
[sectref {OBJECT CONFIGURATION}].


[call [arg objectName] [method destroy]]

This method destroys the object it is invoked for.
If the [option -file] option was given at object creation,
the output file will be finished and closed.


[call [arg objectName] [method startPage] [opt [arg "option value"]...]]

This method starts a new page in the document. The page will have the
default page settings for the document unless overridden by [arg "option"].
See [sectref {PAGE CONFIGURATION}] for page settings.
This will end any ongoing page.

[call [arg objectName] [method endPage]]

This method ends a page in the document.  It is normally not needed since
it is implied by e.g. [method startPage] and [method finish].  However,
if the document is built page by page in e.g. an event driven environment
it can be good to call [method endPage] explicitly to have all the page's
work finished before reentering the event loop.

[call [arg objectName] [method startXObject] [opt [arg "option value"]...]]

This method starts a new XObject in the document.  An XObject is a reusable
drawing object and behaves just like a page where you can draw any graphics.
An XObject must be created between pages and this method will end any ongoing
page. The return value is an id that can be used with [method putImage] to draw
it on the current page or with some forms.
All page settings ([sectref {PAGE CONFIGURATION}]) are
valid when creating an XObject. Default options are
[option -paper] = {100p 100p}, [option -landscape] = 0,
[option -orient] = document default, [option -margin]= 0.

[list_begin options]

[opt_def -noimage [arg bool]]
If this is set the XObject is not added to the image resource set and cannot
be used with putImage, only in forms. The XObject also gets access to resources
which is needed to use e.g. fonts within the XObject. This behaviour has shown
to be PDF reader dependent, and it is currently not known if this can be
made to work better.

[list_end]

[para]
Since 0.9.4.46 the content of an XObject can carry structure elements:
[method tagBegin] works inside one, MCIDs are scoped to the XObject's content
stream, the XObject dictionary gets its own [const /StructParents] and every
[const /MCR] naming that content carries [const /Stm] (ISO 32000-1 clause
14.7.4.4). Two conditions apply.

[para]
An XObject whose content is tagged may be drawn only once -- one structure
tree cannot describe two appearances, and [const /Pg] would have to name two
pages. Drawing it twice raises an error when the document is written. Beyond
that, PDF/UA does not allow a form XObject to be reused at all, tagged or not;
[cmd write] reports this through [var ::pdf4tcl::warnings]. See
[sectref {TAGGED PDF}] and doc/en/reference/TAGGED.md.

[para]
Structure elements enter the tree when [method tagBegin] runs, not when the
XObject is placed. An XObject built at the top of a script therefore lands at
the top of the reading order -- put a heading in one and the tree may start
with an H2, which fails PDF/UA. Keep headings out of XObjects unless the
XObject is built where its content belongs.

[call [arg objectName] [method endXObject]]

This method ends an XObject definition. It works just like [method endPage].

[call [arg objectName] [method finish]]

This method ends the document.
This will do [method endPage] if needed.
If the [option -file] option was given at object creation,
the output file will be finished and closed.

[call [arg objectName] [method get]]

This method returns the generated pdf.
This will do [method endPage] and [method finish] if needed.
If the [option -file] option was given at object creation, nothing is returned.

[call [arg objectName] [method write] [opt [arg "-file filename"]] [opt [arg "-chan channel"]] [opt [arg "-dryrun bool"]]]

This method writes the generated pdf to the given [arg "filename"] or [arg "channel"].
If neither [option -file] nor [option -chan] is given, output is written to stdout.
This will do [method endPage] and [method finish] if needed.
If the [option -file] option was given at object creation, an empty file
is created.

[list_begin options]
[opt_def -file [arg filename]]
Write PDF to the given file.
[opt_def -chan [arg channel]]
Write PDF to the given open channel (e.g. from [cmd open], a socket or a
memory channel). The channel must be opened for writing; it is not closed
by this method. Added in version 0.9.4.25 (ported from the AndroWish fork,
contributed by Christian Werner).
[opt_def -dryrun [arg bool]]
If true, return the PDF data as a string without writing to a file and without
modifying the document state. Useful for previewing or testing the output.
Default is false.
[list_end]


[call [arg objectName] [method addForm] [arg "type"] [arg "x"] [arg "y"] [arg "width"] [arg "height"] [opt [arg "option value"]...]]

Add an interactive form field at the given position and size.
Coordinates are in the document's current unit.

Supported types are [arg text], [arg password], [arg checkbutton]
(alias [arg checkbox]), [arg combobox], [arg listbox],
[arg radiobutton], [arg pushbutton], and [arg signature].

[list_begin definitions]

[def "[emph {Common options}]"]
[list_begin options]
[opt_def -id [arg string]]
Unique field ID (alphanumeric). If omitted, one is generated
automatically: [arg {${type}form${n}}] for most types,
[arg {${group}_${value}}] for radiobuttons,
[arg {Signature${n}}] for signatures.

[opt_def -init [arg value]]
Initial value. For [arg text]/[arg password] a string,
for [arg checkbutton] a boolean, for [arg combobox]/[arg listbox]
an item from [arg -options], for [arg radiobutton] a boolean
selecting this button.

[opt_def -readonly [arg boolean]]
If true, the field is read-only (PDF Ff bit 1). Default [const 0].

[opt_def -required [arg boolean]]
If true, the field is marked as required (PDF Ff bit 2). Default [const 0].
Not valid for [arg pushbutton] or [arg signature].
[list_end]

[def "[emph {Text / Password options}]"]
[list_begin options]
[opt_def -multiline [arg boolean]]
Enable multi-line editing (text only). Default [const 0].

[opt_def -align [arg value]]
Text alignment of the field value: [const left] ([const 0], default),
[const center] ([const 1]) or [const right] ([const 2]), written as the
PDF [const /Q] quadding entry. For [arg text] and [arg password] fields the
generated appearance stream is aligned to match. Also valid for
[arg combobox] and [arg listbox] fields.
[list_end]

[def "[emph {Appearance options}]"]
The following options apply to [arg text], [arg password], [arg combobox] and
[arg listbox] fields. All are optional; the defaults reproduce the previous
output unchanged.
[list_begin options]
[opt_def -color [arg color]]
Text color of the field value, written into the PDF [const /DA] default
appearance string (and the generated appearance stream). Any pdf4tcl color
(RGB list, CMYK list or [const #rrggbb]; colour names require Tk). Default is
black.

[opt_def -borderwidth [arg n]]
Border line width in points, written as [const {/BS << /W n >>}]. [const 0]
(default) means no border.

[opt_def -bordercolor [arg color]]
Border color, written as [const {/MK << /BC ... >>}]. Only takes effect when
[option -borderwidth] is greater than 0. Default [const {0 0 0}].

[opt_def -bgcolor [arg color]]
Background (fill) color of the field, written as [const {/MK << /BG ... >>}].
Default is no background.
[list_end]

[def "[emph {Calculation options}]"]
[list_begin options]
[opt_def -calculate [arg spec]]
Turn a [arg text] field into a calculated field. [arg spec] is a two-element
list [arg {operation fieldnames}], where [arg operation] is one of
[const sum], [const product], [const average], [const min] or [const max] and
[arg fieldnames] is a list of source field ids. Emits an [const {/AA /C}]
calculate action that calls the viewer's built-in [const AFSimple_Calculate],
adds the field to the AcroForm [const /CO] calculation order and sets
[const {/NeedAppearances true}]. Live recalculation requires a
JavaScript-capable viewer (Adobe Acrobat/Reader, Firefox, Chrome/Edge, Foxit);
combine with [option -init] to also show a static, precomputed value in viewers
without JavaScript. Only valid for [arg text] fields.

[opt_def -js [arg spec]]
Attach raw JavaScript actions to a [arg text] field. [arg spec] is a list of
[arg event]/[arg code] pairs, where [arg event] is one of [const calculate]
([const /C]), [const format] ([const /F]), [const validate] ([const /V]) or
[const keystroke] ([const /K]) and [arg code] is the JavaScript body. Useful for
calculations that the built-in [const AFSimple_Calculate] cannot express, e.g.
[const {event.value = this.getField("net").value * 1.19;}]. Shares one
[const /AA] with [option -calculate] / [option -format]; each event may be
defined only once (a clash raises an error). A [const calculate] action adds the
field to the AcroForm [const /CO] order and sets [const {/NeedAppearances true}].
Requires a JavaScript-capable viewer; only valid for [arg text] fields.

[opt_def -format [arg spec]]
Format a numeric [arg text] field via the viewer's built-in
[const AFNumber_Format] / [const AFNumber_Keystroke] ([const {/AA /F}] and
[const {/AA /K}]). [arg spec] is [const number] optionally followed by key/value
pairs: [const decimals] [arg n] (default 2), [const sep] (thousands/decimal
style: [const 0]/[const us], [const 1]/[const plain], [const 2]/[const german],
[const 3]/[const comma]; default 0), [const currency] [arg string] (e.g.
[const { \u20AC}]; non-ASCII is emitted as a JavaScript escape), [const prepend]
[arg boolean] (currency before the number; default false) and [const negred]
[arg boolean] (show negatives in red). Can be combined with [option -calculate]
(both share one [const /AA]). Requires a JavaScript-capable viewer; only valid
for [arg text] fields.
[list_end]

[def "[emph {Checkbutton options}]"]
[list_begin options]
[opt_def -on [arg xobjectId]]
Custom appearance XObject for the checked state.
Created with [method startXObject].

[opt_def -off [arg xobjectId]]
Custom appearance XObject for the unchecked state.
[list_end]

[def "[emph {Combobox / Listbox options}]"]
[list_begin options]
[opt_def -options [arg list]]
List of selectable items. Required for [arg combobox] and [arg listbox].

[opt_def -editable [arg boolean]]
Allow typing custom values (combobox only). Default [const 0].

[opt_def -sort [arg boolean]]
Sort the option list. Default [const 0].

[opt_def -multiselect [arg boolean]]
Allow selecting multiple items (listbox only). Default [const 0].
[list_end]

[def "[emph {Radiobutton options}]"]
[list_begin options]
[opt_def -group [arg string]]
Group name (required, alphanumeric). All buttons sharing the same
group name form a mutually exclusive set.

[opt_def -value [arg string]]
Value for this button (required, alphanumeric). Used as the
PDF appearance state name.
[list_end]

[def "[emph {Pushbutton options}]"]
[list_begin options]
[opt_def -action [arg type]]
Action type: [const reset] (clear all fields), [const url] (open URL),
or [const submit] (submit form data to URL).

[opt_def -url [arg string]]
Target URL. Required when [arg -action] is [const url] or [const submit].

[opt_def -caption [arg string]]
Button label text. Either [arg -action] or [arg -caption] (or both)
must be given.
[list_end]

[def "[emph {Signature options}]"]
[list_begin options]
[opt_def -label [arg string]]
Placeholder text displayed on the signature line.
Default [const Signature]. Only valid for [arg signature] fields.
[list_end]

[def "[emph {Accessibility and tab order options}]"]
[list_begin options]
[opt_def -tooltip [arg string]]
Tooltip text written as PDF [const /TU] (Tooltip User) entry.
Displayed by PDF readers on hover and read aloud by screen readers
(PDF/UA). Valid for all field types.

[opt_def -tabindex [arg integer]]
Tab order index written as PDF [const /TI] entry. Must be a
non-negative integer. Used to define the logical keyboard tab order
within the AcroForm. Valid for all field types.
[list_end]

[list_end]

All fields generate their own appearance streams; the
[const NeedAppearances] flag is never set, ensuring compatibility
with digital signature workflows.
Radiobutton groups are finalized at document write time as a
parent field with [const /Kids] entries.

[list_end]

[subsection {OBJECT METHODS, PAGE}]

[list_begin definitions]

[call [arg objectName] [method getDrawableArea]]

This method returns the size of the available area on the page,
after removing margins.  The return value is a list of width and height,
in the document's default unit.

[call [arg objectName] [method canvas] [arg "path"] [opt [arg "option value"]...]]

Draws the contents of the canvas widget [arg "path"] on the current page.
The return value is the bounding box in pdf page coordinates of the area covered.
Option [arg "-bbox"] gives the area of the canvas to be drawn. Default is
the entire contents, i.e. the result of $path bbox all.
Options [arg "-x"], [arg "-y"], [arg "-width"] and [arg "-height"] defines
an area on the page where to place the contents. Default area starts at origin,
stretching over the drawable area of the page.
Option [arg "-sticky"] defines how to place the contents within the area.
The area is always filled in one direction, preserving aspect ratio, unless
[arg "-sticky"] defines that the other direction should be filled too.  Default
[arg "-sticky"] is [arg "nw"].
If option [arg "-bg"] is true, a background is drawn in the canvas' background
color.  Otherwise only objects are drawn.  Default is false.
Option [arg "-fontmap"] gives a dictionary mapping from Tk font names to PDF font names.
Option [arg "-textscale"] overrides the automatic downsizing made for tk::canvas text items that are deemed too large. If [arg "-textscale"] is larger than 1, all text items are reduced in size by that factor.
[para]
Fonts:
[para]
If no font mapping is given, fonts for text items are limited to PDF's
builtins, i.e. Helvetica, Times and Courier. A guess is made to chose which
one to use to get a reasonable display on the page.
[para]
An element in a font mapping must exactly match the -font option in the
text item. The corresponding mapping value is a PDF font family, e.g. one
created by [cmd pdf4tcl::createFont], possibly followed by a size.
It is recommended to use named fonts
in Tk to control the font mapping in detail.
[para]
Limitations:
[para]
Option [option -splinesteps] for lines/polygons is not applicable: PDF uses
exact cubic Bezier curves ([cmd CanvasBezier]), which are mathematically
equivalent to any number of spline steps. The visual result is equal to or
better than Tk's screen rendering.
[para]
[emph {Stipple:}]
Both offset forms are supported: [option {x,y}] (absolute pixel offset) and
[option {#x,y}] (bitmap-relative -- aligns bitmap pixel (x,y) with canvas
origin). Empty offset (no adjustment) is handled correctly. Minor visual
differences from on-screen rendering may remain due to pattern scaling.
[para]
[emph {Widget classes supported:}]
[const tk::canvas] (class [const Canvas]) -- all standard item types:
rectangle, oval, line, polygon, arc, text, image, bitmap, window.
[para]
[const tkpath] ([cmd ::tkp::canvas], class [const PathCanvas]) -- tkpath
item types: pimage, ptext, pline, polyline, ppolygon, prect, circle,
ellipse, path, group. Handled via [method itempdf] delegation (C-level).
[para]
[const tko::path] (class [const tko::path]) -- tko::path item types:
image, text, line, polyline, polygon, rect, circle, ellipse, path, group,
window. Handled via [method itempdf] delegation.
[para]
[emph {Window items (tk::canvas):}]
The embedded widget must be visible on-screen when the canvas is exported.
Package [package Img] with [option {-format window}] support is required to
capture the widget as a raster image. If [package Img] is not available or
the widget is unmapped, a solid black rectangle is drawn in its place.
[para]
[emph {Window items (tko::path):}]
The item is silently skipped -- no crash, no error message (fix in 0.9.4.24,
BUG-C1). All other items on the same [const tko::path] are exported correctly.
Raster capture of embedded widgets is not supported for [const tko::path].

[call [arg objectName] [method metadata] [opt [arg "option value"]...]]

This method sets metadata fields for this document.  Supported field options are
[arg -author], [arg -creator], [arg -keywords], [arg -producer], [arg -subject],
[arg -title], [arg -creationdate] and [arg -moddate].
Multiple keywords should be passed as a comma-separated string,
e.g. [arg {-keywords "tcl,pdf,document"}].
For [arg -creationdate] and [arg -moddate] a [const {clock seconds}] value is expected.
A value of [const 0] uses the current date and time.
Unknown option names cause an error.

[call [arg objectName] [method bookmarkAdd] [opt [arg "option value"]...]]

Add a bookmark on the current page.

[list_begin options]

[opt_def -title [arg text]]
Set the text of the bookmark.

[opt_def -level [arg level]]
Set the level of the bookmark. Default is 0.

[opt_def -closed [arg boolean]]
Select if the bookmark is closed by default. Default is false, i.e. not closed.

[list_end]

[call [arg objectName] [method embedFile] [arg "filename"] [opt [arg "option value"]...]]

This method embeds a file into the PDF stream. File data is considered binary. Returns an id that can be used in subsequent calls to [method attachFile].

[list_begin options]

[opt_def -id [arg id]]
Explicitly select an id for the file. The [arg "id"] must be unique within the document.

[opt_def -contents [arg data]]
Provides the file contents instead of reading the actual file.
[list_end]

[call [arg objectName] [method attachFile] [arg "x"] [arg "y"] [arg "width"] [arg "height"] [arg "fid"] [arg "description"] [opt [arg "option value"]...]]

This method adds a file annotation to the current page. The location of the file annotation is given by the coordinates [arg "x"], [arg "y"], [arg "width"], [arg "height"]. The annotation is rendered by default as a paperclip icon, which allows the extraction of the attached file. An [arg "fid"] from a previous call to [method embedFile] must be set as well as a [arg "description"], which is shown by the PDF viewer upon activating the annotation.

[list_begin options]

[opt_def -icon [arg icon]]
Controls the appearance of the attachment. Valid values are Paperclip, Tag, Graph, or PushPin. Default value is Paperclip.

[list_end]

[example {
set fid [$pdfobject embedFile "data.txt" -contents "This should be stored in the file."]
$pdfobject attachFile 0 0 100 100 $fid "This is the description"
}]

[call [arg objectName] [method addEmbeddedFile] [arg "filename"] [opt [arg "option value"]...]]

This method embeds a file silently into the PDF document via the Catalog
[const /Names] / [const /EmbeddedFiles] NameTree (ISO 32000 SS7.11.4).
No visible annotation is created on any page.
This is the correct mechanism for electronic invoice attachments
(ZUGFeRD, Factur-X) and other document-level file attachments.
[para]
[arg filename] is used as the display name stored in the PDF
([const /F] and [const /UF] entries of the FileSpec dictionary).
When [option -contents] is not given, the file is read from disk using
[arg filename] as path; in that case the basename is used as the PDF name.
[para]
[strong "PDF/A-1 restriction:"] embedded files are forbidden by
ISO 19005-1 SS6.1.7.  Calling this method when [option -pdfa] is
set to [const 1b] raises an error.  PDF/A-2b and PDF/A-3b allow
embedded files.

[list_begin options]

[opt_def -contents [arg data]]
Raw binary content of the file.  If omitted, the file is read from
disk using [arg filename] as path.

[opt_def -mimetype [arg type]]
MIME type string, e.g. [const "application/xml"].
Written as [const /Subtype] in the EmbeddedFile stream dictionary.

[opt_def -description [arg text]]
Human-readable description stored as [const /Desc] in the FileSpec
dictionary.

[opt_def -afrelationship [arg rel]]
PDF/A-3 [const /AFRelationship] entry.  Valid values are
[const Alternative], [const Data], [const Source],
[const Supplement], and [const Unspecified].

[list_end]

[example {
# ZUGFeRD / Factur-X pattern
set xmlData [read [open "factur-x.xml" rb]]
$pdfobject addEmbeddedFile "factur-x.xml" \
    -contents $xmlData \
    -mimetype "application/xml" \
    -description "Factur-X invoice" \
    -afrelationship Alternative
}]

[call [arg objectName] [method facturx] [opt [arg "option value"]...]]

Attaches an electronic invoice as Factur-X / ZUGFeRD and writes the XMP
metadata the standard requires. This does in one call what
[method addEmbeddedFile] plus [method metadata] would do by hand, and it
adds the [const fx:] XMP block a validating reader looks for.

A valid electronic invoice is PDF/A-3 ([option -pdfa] set to [const 3b]
or [const 3a]); the method does not enforce that, but a file written
without it will not pass an invoice validator. PDF/A-1 forbids embedded
files outright, so [method addEmbeddedFile] refuses there.

Either [option -contents] or [option -file] has to be given, not both.

[list_begin options]

[opt_def -contents [arg data]]
The invoice XML itself.

[opt_def -file [arg path]]
Path to the invoice XML, read from disk. Excludes [option -contents].

[opt_def -filename [arg name]]
The name the XML carries inside the PDF, used for the embedded file and
for [const DocumentFileName] in the XMP
(default: [const factur-x.xml]).

[opt_def -conformance [arg level]]
The invoice profile: [const MINIMUM], [const "BASIC WL"], [const BASIC],
[const "EN 16931"], [const EXTENDED] or [const XRECHNUNG]
(default: [const "EN 16931"]).

[opt_def -documenttype [arg type]]
[const DocumentType] in the XMP (default: [const INVOICE]).

[opt_def -version [arg v]]
The Factur-X version (default: [const 1.0]).

[opt_def -afrelationship [arg rel]]
The [const /AFRelationship] of the embedded XML
(default: [const Alternative]). MINIMUM and BASIC WL are not a complete
invoice on their own and take [const Data].

[opt_def -namespace [arg uri]]
The XMP namespace (default: the Factur-X 1.0 / ZUGFeRD 2.1 URI).

[opt_def -prefix [arg p]]
The XMP prefix (default: [const fx]).

[list_end]

[example {
# Every font has to be embedded -- PDF/A refuses the standard 14, so a
# document set in Helvetica fails validation whatever else is right.
pdf4tcl::loadBaseTrueTypeFont BaseSans "FreeSans.ttf"
pdf4tcl::createFontSpecCID BaseSans Uni

set pdfobject [::pdf4tcl::new %AUTO% -paper a4 -pdfa 3b]
$pdfobject startPage
$pdfobject setFont 12 Uni
...
$pdfobject facturx -file "factur-x.xml" -conformance "EN 16931"
$pdfobject write -file invoice.pdf
# verapdf -f 3b invoice.pdf  ->  PASS
}]
[call [arg objectName] [method orderx] [opt [arg "option value"]...]]

Attaches an electronic order as Order-X, the ordering counterpart of
Factur-X. Same mechanism and the same PDF/A-3 requirement; what differs is
the namespace, the file name and the document types.

[list_begin options]
[opt_def -contents [arg data]]
[opt_def -file [arg path]]
The order XML. One of the two, not both.

[opt_def -filename [arg name]]
Default [const order-x.xml].

[opt_def -conformance [arg level]]
[const basic], [const comfort] or [const extended]
(default: [const comfort]). These are not the Factur-X profiles -- an
invoice level such as [const "EN 16931"] is refused here, and
[const comfort] is refused by [method facturx].

[opt_def -documenttype [arg type]]
[const ORDER], [const ORDER_CHANGE] or [const ORDER_RESPONSE]
(default: [const ORDER]).

[opt_def -version [arg v]]
Default [const 1.0].

[opt_def -afrelationship [arg rel]]
Default [const Alternative]; a partial order takes [const Data].
[list_end]

[example {
set pdfobject [::pdf4tcl::new %AUTO% -paper a4 -pdfa 3b]
$pdfobject startPage
...
$pdfobject orderx -file "order-x.xml" -documenttype ORDER
$pdfobject write -file order.pdf
}]

Everything is delegated to [method facturx], so the two cannot drift
apart: attachment, XMP block and [const /AF] relationship are written by
one piece of code.

[call [arg objectName] [method hyperlinkAdd] [arg "x"] [arg "y"] [arg "width"] [arg "height"] [arg "url"] [opt [arg "option value"]...]]

This method adds a URI hyperlink annotation to the current page. The clickable
area is defined by [arg "x"], [arg "y"], [arg "width"] and [arg "height"]. The
[arg "url"] argument must be a valid URI string (e.g. [const "https://www.example.com"]).
By default the annotation has no visible border.

[list_begin options]

[opt_def -borderwidth [arg n]]
Width of the annotation border in points. Use [const 0] for an invisible border
(default: [const 0]).

[opt_def -bordercolor [arg color]]
Color of the annotation border. Accepts any color format supported by pdf4tcl
(default: [const "{0 0 1}"], blue).

[opt_def -borderradius [arg n]]
Corner radius of the annotation border in points (default: [const 0]).

[opt_def -borderdash [arg "on off"]]
Dash pattern of the border as a list of two numbers [arg on] and [arg off] in
points. An empty list produces a solid border (default: [const {}]).

[opt_def -highlight [arg mode]]
Visual effect when the user clicks the annotation. Valid values are [const N]
(None), [const I] (Invert, default), [const O] (Outline), and [const P] (Push).

[list_end]

[example {
# Invisible link
$pdfobject hyperlinkAdd 50 100 200 20 "https://sourceforge.net/p/pdf4tcl"

# Link with visible blue border
$pdfobject hyperlinkAdd 50 130 200 20 "https://github.com/gregnix/pdf4tcl" \
    -borderwidth 1 -bordercolor {0 0 1}

# Dashed border, rounded corners
$pdfobject hyperlinkAdd 50 160 200 20 "https://www.tcl.tk" \
    -borderwidth 1 -bordercolor {0 0.6 0} -borderdash {5 3} -borderradius 4
}]

[call [arg objectName] [method addAnnotNote] [arg "x"] [arg "y"] [arg "width"] [arg "height"] [opt [arg "option value"]...]]

Add a [const /Text] sticky note annotation (0.9.4.23+).
The annotation appears as a small icon; clicking it opens a popup window.

[emph Note:] Popup behaviour varies between PDF viewers -- some keep the popup
open, others do not show a close button. For consistent cross-viewer display
use [method addAnnotFreeText] instead.

[list_begin options]
[opt_def -content [arg "string"]] Note text shown in the popup.
[opt_def -author  [arg "string"]] Author name shown in the popup header.
[opt_def -subject [arg "string"]] Subject line.
[opt_def -icon    [arg "name"]]   Icon type: [const Note] (default), [const Comment],
[const Key], [const Help], [const NewParagraph], [const Paragraph], [const Insert].
[opt_def -color   [arg "color"]]  Background color (default: [const {1 1 0}] = yellow).
[opt_def -open    [arg "bool"]]   Show popup open by default (default: [const 0]).
[list_end]

[call [arg objectName] [method addAnnotFreeText] [arg "x"] [arg "y"] [arg "width"] [arg "height"] [arg "text"] [opt [arg "option value"]...]]

Add a [const /FreeText] annotation -- a visible text box directly on the page (0.9.4.23+).
No click required; always visible in all viewers.

[list_begin options]
[opt_def -fontsize    [arg "n"]]      Font size in points (default: [const 10]).
[opt_def -color       [arg "color"]]  Text color (default: [const {0 0 0}]).
[opt_def -bgcolor     [arg "color"]]  Background fill color (default: [const {1 1 0.8}]).
[opt_def -borderwidth [arg "n"]]      Border width in points (default: [const 1]).
[opt_def -align       [arg "n"]]      Text alignment: [const 0]=left (default), [const 1]=center, [const 2]=right.
[list_end]

[call [arg objectName] [method addAnnotHighlight] [arg "x"] [arg "y"] [arg "width"] [arg "height"] [opt [arg "option value"]...]]
[call [arg objectName] [method addAnnotUnderline]  [arg "x"] [arg "y"] [arg "width"] [arg "height"] [opt [arg "option value"]...]]
[call [arg objectName] [method addAnnotStrikeOut]  [arg "x"] [arg "y"] [arg "width"] [arg "height"] [opt [arg "option value"]...]]

Text markup annotations (0.9.4.23+). The rectangle defines the text area to mark.

[list_begin options]
[opt_def -color   [arg "color"]] Markup color. Defaults: yellow for Highlight,
black for Underline, red for StrikeOut.
[opt_def -content [arg "string"]] Optional comment text.
[opt_def -author  [arg "string"]] Author name.
[list_end]

[call [arg objectName] [method addAnnotStamp] [arg "x"] [arg "y"] [arg "width"] [arg "height"] [opt [arg "option value"]...]]

Add a [const /Stamp] annotation -- a rubber stamp visible on the page (0.9.4.23+).

[list_begin options]
[opt_def -name    [arg "name"]]   Predefined stamp name (default: [const Draft]).
Valid names: [const Approved], [const Confidential], [const Draft], [const Experimental],
[const Expired], [const Final], [const ForPublicRelease], [const NotApproved],
[const NotForPublicRelease], [const Sold], [const TopSecret].
[opt_def -color   [arg "color"]] Stamp color (default: [const {1 0 0}]).
[opt_def -content [arg "string"]] Optional popup content.
[list_end]

[call [arg objectName] [method addAnnotLine] [arg "x1"] [arg "y1"] [arg "x2"] [arg "y2"] [opt [arg "option value"]...]]

Add a [const /Line] annotation with optional arrowheads (0.9.4.23+).

[list_begin options]
[opt_def -color     [arg "color"]] Line color (default: [const {0 0 0}]).
[opt_def -width     [arg "n"]]     Line width in points (default: [const 1]).
[opt_def -startend  [arg "list"]]  Two-element list of arrowhead styles for
start and end of the line (default: [const {None None}]).
Valid styles: [const None], [const OpenArrow], [const ClosedArrow], [const Square],
[const Circle], [const Diamond], [const Butt], [const Slash].
[opt_def -content   [arg "string"]] Optional popup content.
[list_end]

[call [arg objectName] [method viewerPreferences] [opt [arg "option value"]...]]

Set viewer preference flags in the PDF catalog. These control how a PDF viewer
displays the document when it is opened. Options can be combined freely.

[list_begin options]

[opt_def -pagelayout [arg layout]]
Set the page layout on open. Valid values: [const SinglePage], [const OneColumn],
[const TwoColumnLeft], [const TwoColumnRight], [const TwoPageLeft], [const TwoPageRight].

[opt_def -pagemode [arg mode]]
Set the page mode on open. Valid values: [const UseNone], [const UseOutlines],
[const UseThumbs], [const FullScreen], [const UseOC], [const UseAttachments].

[opt_def -hidetoolbar [arg bool]]
Hide the viewer toolbar when the document is open (default false).

[opt_def -hidemenubar [arg bool]]
Hide the viewer menu bar (default false).

[opt_def -hidewindowui [arg bool]]
Hide viewer interface elements (default false).

[opt_def -fitwindow [arg bool]]
Resize the document window to fit the first page (default false).

[opt_def -centerwindow [arg bool]]
Center the document window on the screen (default false).

[opt_def -displaydoctitle [arg bool]]
Display the document title from metadata instead of the file name (default false).

[opt_def -nonfullscreenpagemode [arg mode]]
Page mode when leaving full-screen mode. Valid values: [const UseNone],
[const UseOutlines], [const UseThumbs], [const UseOC].

[opt_def -direction [arg dir]]
Reading order. Valid values: [const L2R] (default) or [const R2L] (right-to-left).

[opt_def -printscaling [arg scale]]
Default print scaling. Valid values: [const AppDefault] or [const None].

[opt_def -duplex [arg mode]]
Paper handling when printing. Valid values: [const None], [const Simplex],
[const DuplexFlipShortEdge], [const DuplexFlipLongEdge].

[list_end]

[example {
$pdfobject viewerPreferences -pagemode FullScreen -hidetoolbar 1
$pdfobject viewerPreferences -pagelayout TwoColumnLeft -direction L2R
}]

[call [arg objectName] [method pageLabel] [arg "pageIndex"] [opt [arg "option value"]...]]

Define a page label range starting at the given zero-based [arg pageIndex].
PDF page labels allow viewers to display custom page numbers (e.g. roman numerals
for a preface, decimal numbers for the main body). Multiple ranges can be defined.

[list_begin options]

[opt_def -style [arg style]]
Numbering style. Valid values: [const D] (decimal: 1 2 3), [const r] (roman lowercase: i ii iii),
[const R] (roman uppercase: I II III), [const a] (alpha lowercase: a b c),
[const A] (alpha uppercase: A B C), or empty string (prefix only, no number).

[opt_def -prefix [arg string]]
Label prefix prepended to each page number (e.g. [const "App-"] produces App-1, App-2...).

[opt_def -start [arg integer]]
Start value for the numbering in this range. Must be a positive integer. Default is 1.

[list_end]

[example {
# Preface pages: i, ii, iii...
$pdfobject pageLabel 0 -style r

# Main body pages: 1, 2, 3...
$pdfobject pageLabel 4 -style D -start 1

# Appendix with prefix
$pdfobject pageLabel 20 -style A -prefix "App-"
}]

[list_end]

[subsection {OBJECT METHODS, TEXT}]

[list_begin definitions]

[call [arg objectName] [method setFont] [arg "size"] [opt [arg "fontname"]]]

This method sets the font used by text drawing routines. If [arg "fontname"]
is not provided, the previously set [arg "fontname"] is kept.

[call [arg objectName] [method getStringWidth] [arg "str"] [opt [arg options]]]

This method returns the width of [arg str] in the current unit.
Options (0.9.4.23+):
[list_begin options]
[opt_def -font [arg fontName]] Use [arg fontName] instead of the current font.
[opt_def -size [arg fontSize]] Use [arg fontSize] instead of the current font size.
[opt_def -internal [arg bool]] Return width in points instead of current unit.
[list_end]
Without options the method behaves as before.
If no font has been set and [option -font] is not given, an error is raised.

[call [arg objectName] [method getCharWidth] [arg "char"]]

This method returns the width of a character under the current font.

[call [arg objectName] [method inPage]]

Returns [const 1] if a page is currently open, [const 0] otherwise.
Useful for library code managing page state without accessing internals.

[call [arg objectName] [method currentPage]]

Returns the current page number (1-based).
Returns [const 0] before the first [method startPage] call.

[call [arg objectName] [method pageCount]]

Returns the number of completed pages ([method endPage] called).
A currently open page is not counted until [method endPage].

[call [arg objectName] [method setTextPosition] [arg "x"] [arg "y"]]

Set coordinate for next text command.

[call [arg objectName] [method moveTextPosition] [arg "dx"] [arg "dy"]]

Increment position by [arg "dx"], [arg "dy"] for the next text command.

[call [arg objectName] [method getTextPosition]]

This method returns the current text coordinate.

[call [arg objectName] [method newLine] [opt [arg spacing]]]

Moves text coordinate down and resets x to where the latest
[method setTextPosition] was. The number of lines to move down can
be set by [arg spacing]. This may be any real number, including negative,
and defaults to the value set by [method setLineSpacing].

[call [arg objectName] [method setLineSpacing] [arg spacing]]

Set the default line spacing used be e.g. [method newLine].  Initially
the spacing is 1.

[call [arg objectName] [method getLineSpacing]]

Get the current default line spacing factor (a dimensionless multiplier).

[call [arg objectName] [method setKerning] [arg value]]

Pair kerning, which is the per-pair spacing correction a font ships for
pairs like AV or To. Three levels:

[list_begin definitions]
[def [const 0]] Off. One [const Tj] per string, no adjustments, and
[method getStringWidth] adds up advance widths alone. This is the output
of 0.9.4.46 and earlier.
[def [const 1]] Embedded fonts only. The default.
[def [const all]] The standard 14 as well.
[list_end]

The amounts come from three sources, whichever the face carries: the
legacy [const kern] table, the [const kern] feature of [const GPOS]
(pair adjustment, both subtable formats, including extension lookups),
and for the standard 14 the Adobe metrics. Of 66 TrueType faces on a
typical Linux installation, 33 carry a [const kern] table and 43 a
[const kern] feature in GPOS, so both are needed.

The standard 14 are not in the default because their output is pinned by
the regression tests. Turning them on changes documents that have looked
the same for years. [method setKerning] [const all] raises an error when
the pairs are not available, rather than silently doing nothing.

A kerned string is written as a [const TJ] array instead of a
[const Tj] string, and [method getStringWidth] returns the kerned width,
so measuring and drawing agree. Where a face has no pairs for a string,
the plain form is written and the output is unchanged.

Of [const lookupFlag], bit 3 ([const IgnoreMarks]) is honoured: where a
face sets it, mark glyphs named by [const GDEF] are skipped, so that
[const A] followed by a combining accent and [const V] is kerned as the
pair [const AV]. 18 of 66 faces on a typical Linux installation set that
bit, and none sets any other.

What is not read: the remaining [const lookupFlag] bits, script and
language system selection (every [const kern] feature is taken),
contextual lookups, and version 1 of the [const kern] table (the Apple
layout, skipped rather than misread).

[call [arg objectName] [method getKerning]]

Get the current kerning level: [const 0], [const 1] or [const all].

[call [arg objectName] [method setLigatures] [arg onOff]]

Standard ligatures from the [const liga] feature of [const GSUB]:
[const fi], [const fl], [const ffi] and whatever else the face defines.
Off by default, because it changes the output of existing documents.

Only for embedded fonts, and only lookup type 4 (ligature substitution),
including extension lookups. The longest match wins, so [const ffi]
becomes one glyph rather than [const f] followed by [const fi].

The [const ToUnicode] CMap records every character a ligature stands for,
so a reader extracting the text gets [const fi] back and searching for a
word still finds it. This is the part that has to be right: a ligature
that leaves text unsearchable is worse than no ligature.

Not every face means the same thing by [const liga]. Of 66 TrueType faces
on a typical Linux installation, one carries 424 ligature targets,
another none at all, and DejaVu Sans has an Arabic [const liga] feature
and no [const fi] glyph. Check the face before relying on this.

Like [method setKerning], the setting belongs to the text state and is
saved by [method gsave] with the rest.

[call [arg objectName] [method getLigatures]]

Get whether ligatures are applied: [const 0] or [const 1].

[call [arg objectName] [method getLineHeight]]

Get the actual vertical distance advanced by [method newLine] in the document's
current unit. This is [arg font_size] times the line spacing factor. Use this to
calculate bounding boxes around multi-line text blocks. Requires a font to be set.

[call [arg objectName] [method text] [arg "str"] [opt [arg "option value"]...]]

Draw text at the position defined by setTextPosition using the font defined by 
setFont.
   [list_begin options]
      [opt_def -align "[arg left|right|center]   (default left)"]
      [opt_def -angle "[arg degrees]   (default 0) - Orient string at the specified angle."]  
      [opt_def -xangle "[arg degrees]   (default 0)"]
      [opt_def -yangle "[arg degrees]   (default 0) - Apply x or y shear to the text."]
      [opt_def -x "[arg x]   (default 0)"]
      [opt_def -y "[arg y]   (default 0) - Allow the text to be positioned without setTextPosition."]
      [opt_def -bg "[arg bool|color]   (default 0)"]
      [opt_def -background "[arg bool|color]"]
      [opt_def -fill "[arg bool|color]"]
         Any of [option -bg], [option -background] or [option -fill] cause the text to be drawn
         on a filled background. If a boolean true is given, the background color is taken from
         [method setBgColor]. Alternatively, a color value in any format accepted by pdf4tcl
         can be given directly (e.g. [const "{1 0 0}"] for red). All three options are aliases.
   [list_end]


[call [arg objectName] [method drawTextBox] [arg "x"] [arg "y"] [arg "width"] [arg "height"] [arg "str"] [opt [arg "option value"]...]]

Draw the text string [arg "str"] wrapping at blanks and tabs so that it fits within the box defined
by [arg "x"], [arg "y"], [arg "width"] and [arg "height"]. An embedded newline in [arg "str"] causes
a new line in the output. If [arg "str"] is too long to fit in the specified box, it is truncated and the unused remainder is returned.
   [list_begin options]
      [opt_def -align "[arg left|right|center|justify]"]
         Specifies the justification. If not given, the text is left justified.
      [opt_def -linesvar "[arg var]"]
         Gives the name of a variable which will be set to the number of lines written.
      [opt_def -newyvar "[arg var]"]
         Gives the name of a variable which will be set to the Y position after
         the last rendered line, in the current unit and in the same
         coordinate system as the [arg y] passed in -- that is, following
         [option -orient] (0.9.4.23+, corrected in 0.9.4.44: before that it
         reported the internal page coordinate).
         Allows the caller to continue drawing below the text box:
[example {
$pdf drawTextBox $x $y $w $h $text -newyvar nextY
$pdf line $x $nextY [expr {$x+$w}] $nextY
}]
      [opt_def -dryrun "[arg bool]"]
         If true, no changes will be made to the PDF document. The return
         value and [option -linesvar] gives information of what would happen
         with the given text.
   [list_end]


[call [arg objectName] [method getFontMetric] [arg "metric"]]

Get information about current font. The available [arg "metric"]s are
[option ascend], [option descend], [option fixed], [option bboxb],
[option bboxt] and [option height].

[list_begin options]

[opt_def ascend]
Top of typical glyph, displacement from anchor point. Typically a positive number since it is above the anchor point.
[opt_def descend]
Bottom of typical glyph, displacement from anchor point. Typically a negative number since it is below the anchor point.
[opt_def fixed]
Boolean which is true if this is a fixed width font.
[opt_def bboxb]
Bottom of Bounding Box, displacement from anchor point. Typically a negative number since it is below the anchor point.
[opt_def bboxt]
Top of Bounding Box, displacement from anchor point. Typically a positive number since it is above the anchor point.
[opt_def height]
Height of font's Bounding Box.

[list_end]

[list_end]

[subsection {OBJECT METHODS, TAGGED PDF}]

An ordinary PDF records only where glyphs are painted. Nothing in the file
says which run of glyphs is a heading, a table cell or a page number, and the
reading order is whatever order the content stream happens to use, which is
wrong as soon as a page has more than one column.

A tagged PDF adds a tree of structure elements next to the content and links
the two, as described by ISO 32000-1 clause 14.7 and 14.8. Assistive
technology walks that tree instead of the paint order; it is also what makes
text extraction, reflow and structure preserving export to HTML reliable.
Tagging is required by PDF/UA (ISO 14289) and by the [const a] conformance
levels of PDF/A.

Tagging is off by default and costs nothing when unused. The methods below
are available from 0.9.4.36 on.

[para]
Being tagged is not the same as being accessible. A document in which every
paragraph is [const /P] and every heading is [const /H1] is formally tagged
and validates cleanly, but tells a reader nothing useful.

[list_begin definitions]

[call [arg objectName] [method tagged] [opt [arg boolean]] [opt [arg options]]]

Enables or disables tagging. Must be called before the first
[method tagBegin]. Raises the PDF version to 1.4. If no structure element is
ever opened, no structure tree is written at all.

[list_begin options]
[opt_def -lang [arg tag]] Natural language of the document as an RFC 3066
tag, for example [const de-DE]. Written as [const /Lang] in the document
catalog.
[opt_def -ua [arg part]] Writes the PDF/UA identification schema
([const pdfuaid:part]) into the XMP packet. [arg part] is [const 0] (the
default, no claim), [const 1] or [const 2]. When [option -pdfa] is set as
well, the namespace is additionally declared through a
[const pdfaExtension] schema, which PDF/A requires for any property outside
the predefined ones -- so a document can be archivable and accessible at the
same time.
[list_end]

[option -ua] is deliberately opt-in and not implied by enabling tagging. The
entry asserts conformance that pdf4tcl cannot verify: embedded font programs,
a document title, tagging of [emph all] content and a sensible heading order
are the caller's responsibility. A document that claims PDF/UA and then fails
validation is worse than one that claims nothing.

[call [arg objectName] [method tagBegin] [arg "type"] [opt [arg options]]]

Opens a structure element. Everything painted until the matching
[method tagEnd] belongs to it. Elements nest, and the nesting of the calls is
the nesting of the tree.

[para]
[arg type] must be one of the standard structure types of ISO 32000-1
Table 333 to 337:
[const Document], [const Part], [const Art], [const Sect], [const Div],
[const BlockQuote], [const Caption], [const TOC], [const TOCI],
[const Index], [const NonStruct], [const Private],
[const P], [const H], [const H1] to [const H6],
[const L], [const LI], [const Lbl], [const LBody],
[const Table], [const TR], [const TH], [const TD], [const THead],
[const TBody], [const TFoot],
[const Span], [const Quote], [const Note], [const Reference],
[const BibEntry], [const Code], [const Figure], [const Formula] and
[const Form].
Any other type would require a [const /RoleMap] entry and is refused rather
than written out and then silently ignored by readers.

[para]
Where the standard fixes the parent of a type, a call that would put it
somewhere else is refused as well:

[list_begin definitions]
[def "[const LI] in [const L]"]
[def "[const LBody] in [const LI]"]
[def "[const THead], [const TBody], [const TFoot] in [const Table]"]
[def "[const TR] in [const Table], [const THead], [const TBody] or [const TFoot]"]
[def "[const TH], [const TD] in [const TR]"]
[def "[const TOCI] in [const TOC]"]
[list_end]

Every other type may appear anywhere. [const NonStruct] carries no structural
meaning of its own (ISO 32000-1 clause 14.8.4.2) and is skipped when the
parent is determined, so a row wrapped in one is still a row. A refused call
raises an error before the element exists and leaves the tree unchanged.

[para]
This closes a class of defect that goes unreported otherwise: a validator
checks conformance, not sense, and a reader repairs nothing. A cell outside
a row passes every check and is announced as a table without rows.

[para]
[method tagEnd] checks the other direction and refuses to close a container
the standard requires to hold something:

[list_begin definitions]
[def "[const L] holds at least one [const LI]"]
[def "[const LI] holds an [const LBody]"]
[def "[const Table] holds [const TR], [const THead], [const TBody] or [const TFoot]"]
[def "[const THead], [const TBody], [const TFoot] hold at least one [const TR]"]
[def "[const TR] holds at least one [const TH] or [const TD]"]
[list_end]

Marked content does not count here -- a table holding only text is still a
table without rows. A refused [method tagEnd] leaves the element open, so the
missing content can be added and the element closed afterwards.

[para]
An element closed without any content at all -- no marked content, no child
element and no annotation -- is reported in [var ::pdf4tcl::warnings] rather
than refused: the standard permits it, but such an element designates
nothing. [const TD] and [const TH] are exempt, because a blank cell belongs
in the tree; without it the column mapping shifts. An element that holds only
an annotation, such as a [const Link] or a [const Form], is not empty --
these consist of an [const /OBJR] and never carry an MCID.

[list_begin options]
[opt_def -alt [arg text]] Alternative description, written as
[const /Alt]. For a [const Figure] this is the only text a screen reader can
announce.
[opt_def -actualtext [arg text]] Replacement text for extraction, written as
[const /ActualText]. Use it where the painted glyphs differ from the
characters they represent, for example a ligature drawn as one glyph.
[opt_def -title [arg text]] Human readable label, written as [const /T].
[opt_def -lang [arg tag]] Marks a passage in a language other than the
document language.
[opt_def -listnumbering [arg style]] Applies to [const L] only and writes
[const {/A <</O /List /ListNumbering ...>>}]. [arg style] is [const None],
[const Disc], [const Circle], [const Square], [const Decimal],
[const UpperRoman], [const LowerRoman], [const UpperAlpha] or
[const LowerAlpha]. Lets a reader announce the list style instead of reading
the painted bullet glyph.
[opt_def -id [arg name]] Identifier of a table cell, referenced by
[option -headers] of the cells it applies to.
[opt_def -headers [arg list]] Applies to [const TH] and [const TD] and names
the header cells for this cell by their [option -id]. Together with
[option -scope] this covers the requirement of ISO 14289-1 clause 7.5;
[option -headers] is the one that works for irregular tables, where the
relation cannot be derived from the layout.
[opt_def -scope [arg scope]] Applies to [const TH] only and writes
[const {/A <</O /Table /Scope ...>>}]. [arg scope] is [const Row],
[const Column] or [const Both]. ISO 14289-1 clause 7.5 requires it wherever
the relation between a header cell and its data cells cannot be derived
algorithmically.
[list_end]

Text passed to these options is written as a UTF-16BE string when it contains
anything outside printable ASCII, so it is not restricted to Latin-1 the way
[method bookmarkAdd] titles are.

[call [arg objectName] [method tagEnd]]

Closes the innermost open structure element. Raises an error if no element is
open. All elements must be closed before the document is finished.

[call [arg objectName] [method tagText] [arg "type"] [arg "str"] [opt [arg options]]]

Convenience for a complete element around a single [method text] call.
Options are split by name: [option -alt], [option -actualtext],
[option -title], [option -lang] and [option -scope] go to [method tagBegin],
everything else to [method text]. Returns whatever [method text] returns.

[call [arg objectName] [method tagArtifact] [opt [arg options]]]

[para]
Unlike [method tagBegin], this works inside an XObject as well. An artifact
never carries an MCID, because nothing refers to it -- no parent tree entry
and no [const /Stm] in an [const /MCR]. For a purely decorative block that is
the correct and only marking required.

[para]
Content that belongs to neither a structure element nor an artifact is
counted while the document is built and reported once, at [method finish].
ISO 14289-1 clause 7.1 requires every piece of content to be one or the
other, and nothing else in the toolchain notices when it is not: a validator
cannot know which operators were meant to be content. The entry goes to
[var ::pdf4tcl::warnings]; it is a warning and not an error, because
untagged content is legal PDF and a caller may mean it.

[para]
Only operators that put marks on the page count -- path painting, text
showing, XObjects and shadings. Setting a colour, a font or a matrix outside
an element is not a defect. Content inside an XObject does not count either,
since the tag on the [const Do] that places it covers the whole block.

[call [arg objectName] [method getUntaggedCount]]

Number of painting operations so far that belonged to neither a structure
element nor an artifact. Zero is what PDF/UA asks for. Always zero while
tagging is off.
[call [arg objectName] [method tagArtifactEnd]]

Marks content that is not part of the document: running heads, page numbers,
rules, background decoration. Artifacts carry no [const /MCID] and are
skipped by assistive technology (ISO 32000-1 clause 14.8.2.2).

[list_begin options]
[opt_def -type [arg type]] One of [const Pagination], [const Layout],
[const Page] or [const Background].
[opt_def -subtype [arg subtype]] One of [const Header], [const Footer] or
[const Watermark].
[list_end]

Without options a plain [const BMC] artifact is written.

[para]
A structure element may still be open when [method tagArtifact] is called --
a running foot on the second page of a paragraph that spans the page break is
the normal case. The element's marked content is closed first and reopened by
the next painting call, so the artifact sits [emph between] two sequences of
the element rather than inside one. ISO 14289-1 clause 7.1 forbids an
artifact inside tagged content and tagged content inside an artifact.
Opening a structure element while an artifact is open raises an error.

[list_end]

[para]
Marked content is opened at the first painting operation after
[method tagBegin], and only for the innermost open element. A grouping
element such as [const L], [const TR] or [const Table] paints nothing itself
and therefore carries no marked content at all; an element interrupted by a
nested one receives a further marked content sequence when painting resumes.
Elements may span page breaks: the sequence is closed at the end of the page
and a new one opened on the next, because marked content may not cross a
content stream boundary.

[para]
Annotations, that is links created with [method hyperlinkAdd], form fields
from [method addForm] and the [method addAnnot...] family, are attached to
the structure tree when they are created while a [const Link] or
[const Annot] element is open. The element then receives an [const /OBJR]
entry and the annotation a [const /StructParent] key pointing back
(ISO 32000-1 clause 14.7.4.4). An annotation created outside such an element
stays unattached: the link still works when clicked, but assistive technology
cannot reach it. Nothing is inferred automatically, so a link has to be
wrapped explicitly:

[example {
$pdf tagBegin P
$pdf text "Mehr dazu auf der" -x 50 -y 700
$pdf tagBegin Link -alt "pdf4tcl project page"
$pdf tagText Span "Projektseite" -x 155 -y 700
$pdf hyperlinkAdd 155 698 65 14 "https://github.com/gregnix/pdf4tcl"
$pdf tagEnd
$pdf tagEnd
}]

A page carrying annotations is written with [const {/Tabs /S}] while tagging
is enabled, so that tabbing follows the structure tree; untagged documents
keep [const {/Tabs /R}], the row order a plain form wants. ISO 14289-1
clause 7.18.3 requires the former.

[para]
PDF/UA additionally requires a link annotation to carry [const /Contents],
because that is what a reader announces. It is taken from the element's
[option -alt] when the annotation does not already have one; an explicit
[const /Contents] always wins.

[para]
Tagging inside an XObject ([method startPage] with [option -xobject]) is not
supported and raises an error.

[para]
[cmd ::pdf4tcl::catPdf] merges the logical structure of tagged input
documents. Each page carries a [const /StructParents] key indexing the parent
tree of its own document and each document numbers its pages from zero, so
the keys of the appended document are shifted, in the parent tree and in
every [const /StructParents] and [const /StructParent]; the [const /Document]
subtrees then hang under one [const /StructTreeRoot]. MCIDs are not
renumbered, since they are scoped to one page's content stream and pages are
not merged.

[para]
Mixing tagged and untagged input cannot be merged and is reported in
[var ::pdf4tcl::warnings]: appending an untagged document to a tagged one
leaves its pages outside the tree, and appending a tagged document to an
untagged one drops its structure, because adopting it would leave the first
document's pages outside instead. Both results are valid PDF and neither is
PDF/UA conformant.

[para]
Since 0.9.4.44 the interactive form is merged as well: [const /Fields] is the
union of all inputs, [const /DR] is combined per sub-dictionary with the first
document keeping its objects on a name clash, and [const /SigFlags] is the
bitwise or. Field names are deliberately not made unique -- two fields of the
same name are one field with one shared value (ISO 32000-1 clause 12.7.3.2),
which is sometimes intended, and renaming would break the [const /T] reference
in any JavaScript shipped with the document. A collision is reported through
[var ::pdf4tcl::warnings] instead.

[para]
What is still taken from the first document alone is its catalog: the merged
file carries that document's title and [const /Metadata], not a combined one.
Embedded font streams are shared where they are byte-identical -- measured on
two documents using the same CID font, 772348 bytes of input produced 387881
in the merge -- but the font dictionaries around them stay duplicated, because
renumbering makes their bodies differ. That costs a few hundred bytes per
font.

[para]
Example:

[example {
package require pdf4tcl

# PDF/UA requires embedded font programs; the base 14 fonts have none.
pdf4tcl::loadBaseTrueTypeFont BaseFreeSans FreeSans.ttf
pdf4tcl::createFont BaseFreeSans DemoFont iso8859-1

set pdf [pdf4tcl::new %AUTO% -paper a4]
$pdf tagged 1 -lang de-DE -ua 1
$pdf metadata -title "Beispiel"
$pdf viewerPreferences -displaydoctitle 1
$pdf startPage

$pdf tagArtifact -type Pagination -subtype Footer
$pdf setFont 8 DemoFont
$pdf text "Seite 1" -x 500 -y 40
$pdf tagArtifactEnd

$pdf setFont 18 DemoFont
$pdf tagText H1 "Kapitel 1" -x 50 -y 780

$pdf setFont 11 DemoFont
$pdf tagBegin P
$pdf text "Erste Zeile" -x 50 -y 750
$pdf text "Zweite Zeile desselben Absatzes" -x 50 -y 735
$pdf tagEnd

$pdf tagBegin Figure -alt "Rotes Quadrat"
$pdf setFillColor 0.8 0.1 0.1
$pdf rectangle 50 600 60 60 -filled 1
$pdf tagEnd

$pdf write -file tagged.pdf
$pdf destroy
}]

A fuller example with a list, a table and a paragraph spanning a page break
is [file examples/tagged.tcl].

[subsection {OBJECT METHODS, IMAGES}]

A limited set of image formats are directly understood by pdf4tcl,
currently some JPEG, some PNG, and some TIFF formats.
To use unsupported formats, use Tk and the Img
package to load and dump images to raw format which can be fed to
[method putRawImage] and [method addRawImage].

[list_begin definitions]

[call [arg objectName] [method putImage] [arg "id"] [arg "x"] [arg "y"] [opt [arg "option value"]...]]

Put an image on the current page. The image must have been added previously by
[method addImage] or [method addRawImage]. The [arg "id"] is the one returned
from the add command.

[list_begin options]

[opt_def -angle [arg degrees]]
Rotate image [arg degrees] counterclockwise around the anchor point.
Default is 0.

[opt_def -anchor [arg anchor]]
Set the anchor point (nw, n, ne etc.) of the image.
Coordinates [arg "x"] and [arg "y"] places the anchor point, and any rotation is around the anchor point.
Default is nw if [option -orient] is true, otherwise se.

[opt_def -height [arg height]]
Set the height of the image.  Default height is one point per pixel.  If [arg width] is set but not [arg height],
the height is selected to preserve the aspect ratio of the image.

[opt_def -width [arg width]]
Set the width of the image.  Default width is one point per pixel.
If [arg height] is set but not [arg width], the width is selected to
preserve the aspect ratio of the image.

[list_end]

[call [arg objectName] [method putRawImage] [arg "data"] [arg "x"] [arg "y"]  [opt [arg "option value"]...]]
Put an image on the current page. Works like [method putImage] except that the raw image data is given directly.

[list_begin options]

[opt_def -compress [arg boolean]]
Raw data will be zlib compressed if this option is set to true.
Default value is the document's [option -compress] setting.

[list_end]

[example {
  image create photo img1 -file image.gif
  set imgdata [img1 data]
  mypdf putRawImage $imgdata 60 20 -height 40
}]

[call [arg objectName] [method addImage] [arg "filename"] [opt [arg "option value"]...]]

Add an image to the document. Returns an id that can be used in subsequent
calls to [method putImage]. Supported formats are PNG, JPEG and TIFF.
Both non-interlaced and Adam7-interlaced PNG files are accepted; interlaced
PNGs are de-interlaced on load and must have a bit depth of at least 8.
[list_begin options]

[opt_def -id [arg id]]
Explicitly select an id for the image. The [arg "id"] must be unique within the document.

[opt_def -type [arg name]]
Override automatic type detection based on file extension.
Valid values are [const png], [const jpg] (or [const jpeg]), [const tif] (or [const tiff]).
Set the image type.  This can usually be deduced from the file name, this
option helps when that is not possible.  This can be either "png", "jpeg",
or "tiff".
[list_end]

[call [arg objectName] [method addRawImage] [arg "data"] [opt [arg "option value"]...]]
Add an image to the document. Works like [method addImage] except that the raw image data is given directly.

[list_begin options]

[opt_def -compress [arg boolean]]
Raw data will be zlib compressed if this option is set to true.
Default value is the document's [option -compress] setting.

[list_end]

[example {
  image create photo img1 -file image.gif
  set imgdata [img1 data]
  set id [mypdf addRawImage $imgdata]
  mypdf putImage $id 20 60 -width 100
}]

[call [arg objectName] [method getImageHeight] [arg "id"]]

This method returns the height of the image identified by [arg "id"].

[call [arg objectName] [method getImageSize] [arg "id"]]

This method returns the size of the image identified by [arg "id"].  The
return value is a list of width and height.

[call [arg objectName] [method getImageWidth] [arg "id"]]

This method returns the width of the image identified by [arg "id"].

[list_end]

[subsection {OBJECT METHODS, COLORS}]

Colors can be expressed in various formats. First, as a three element list
of values in the range 0.0 to 1.0. Second, in the format #XXXXXX where
the Xes are two hexadecimal digits per color value.  Third, if Tk is available,
any color accepted by winfo rgb is accepted.

[list_begin definitions]

[call [arg objectName] [method setBgColor] [arg "red"] [arg "green"] [arg "blue"]]
Sets the background color for text operations where -bg is true.

[call [arg objectName] [method setBgColor] [arg "c"] [arg "m"] [arg "y"] [arg "k"]]
Alternative calling form, to set color in CMYK color space.

[call [arg objectName] [method setFillColor] [arg "red"] [arg "green"] [arg "blue"]]
Sets the fill color for graphics operations, and the foreground color for
text operations.

[call [arg objectName] [method setFillColor] [arg "c"] [arg "m"] [arg "y"] [arg "k"]]
Alternative calling form, to set color in CMYK color space.

[call [arg objectName] [method setStrokeColor] [arg "red"] [arg "green"] [arg "blue"]]
Sets the stroke color for graphics operations.

[call [arg objectName] [method setStrokeColor] [arg "c"] [arg "m"] [arg "y"] [arg "k"]]
Alternative calling form, to set color in CMYK color space.

[call [arg objectName] [method setAlpha] [arg "value"]]
Sets the opacity for both fill and stroke operations.
[arg value] must be a number between 0.0 (fully transparent) and 1.0 (fully
opaque). Values outside this range are clamped. The default is 1.0.
Internally this creates a PDF ExtGState object with [const /ca] (fill alpha)
and [const /CA] (stroke alpha). Identical alpha values are cached and reuse
the same ExtGState object. The alpha state is saved and restored by
[method gsave] / [method grestore].

[call [arg objectName] [method setAlpha] [arg "value"] [option "-fill"]]
Sets fill opacity only, leaving stroke opacity unchanged.

[call [arg objectName] [method setAlpha] [arg "value"] [option "-stroke"]]
Sets stroke opacity only, leaving fill opacity unchanged.

[call [arg objectName] [method setAlpha] [arg "fillValue"] [arg "strokeValue"]]
Sets fill and stroke opacity independently in a single call.

[call [arg objectName] [method getAlpha]]
Returns the current opacity values as a two-element list
[arg "fillAlpha strokeAlpha"].

[call [arg objectName] [method setBlendMode] [arg "mode"]]
Sets the PDF blend mode for subsequent graphics and text operations.
[arg mode] must be one of: [const Normal], [const Multiply], [const Screen],
[const Overlay], [const Darken], [const Lighten], [const ColorDodge],
[const ColorBurn], [const HardLight], [const SoftLight], [const Difference],
[const Exclusion], [const Hue], [const Saturation], [const Color],
[const Luminosity].
Use [const Normal] to reset to the default. Internally this creates a PDF
ExtGState object combining [const /BM] with the current alpha values; objects
are cached per mode and alpha combination. The PDF version is raised to 1.4
automatically.

[call [arg objectName] [method getBlendMode]]
Returns the currently active blend mode string (default: [const Normal]).

[call [arg objectName] [method linearGradient] [arg "x1"] [arg "y1"] [arg "x2"] [arg "y2"] [arg "color1"] [arg "color2"] [opt [arg "options"]]]
Paints a linear (axial) gradient from ([arg x1],[arg y1]) to
([arg x2],[arg y2]). [arg color1] is the colour at the start point,
[arg color2] at the end point. Both colours can be specified as an RGB triple
[arg "{r g b}"] (values 0.0–1.0), a named colour ([const red], [const green],
[const blue], [const white], [const black], [const yellow], [const cyan],
[const magenta]) or a CSS hex string [arg #rrggbb].
[nl]
Options:
[list_begin options]
[opt_def -extend [arg "{bool bool}"]] Whether to extend the gradient beyond
the start ([arg bool1]) and end ([arg bool2]) coordinates. Default: [const {1 1}].
[list_end]
[nl]
The gradient is painted using PDF ShadingType 2 + FunctionType 2 and
registered in the page [const /Shading] resource dictionary.
Clip the drawing area first (e.g. [method clip] or [method gsave]/[method grestore])
to constrain the fill.

[call [arg objectName] [method radialGradient] [arg "cx1"] [arg "cy1"] [arg "r1"] [arg "cx2"] [arg "cy2"] [arg "r2"] [arg "color1"] [arg "color2"] [opt [arg "options"]]]
Paints a radial gradient between two circles. The first circle is centred at
([arg cx1],[arg cy1]) with radius [arg r1]; the second at ([arg cx2],[arg cy2])
with radius [arg r2]. [arg color1] applies to the first circle,
[arg color2] to the second. Colour formats are the same as for
[method linearGradient].
[nl]
Options: same [option -extend] option as [method linearGradient].
[nl]
Uses PDF ShadingType 3 + FunctionType 2.

[list_end]

[subsection {OBJECT METHODS, GRAPHICS}]

[list_begin definitions]

[call [arg objectName] [method setLineWidth] [arg "width"]]
   Sets the width for subsequent line drawing.
   Line width must be a non-negative number.

[call [arg objectName] [method setLineDash] [opt [arg "on off"]...] [opt [arg "offset"]]]
   Sets the dash pattern for subsequent line drawing.
   Offset and any elements in the dash pattern must be non-negative numbers.
   [emph "on off"] is a series of pairs of numbers which define a 
   dash pattern. The 1st, 3rd ... numbers give units to paint, 
   the 2nd, 4th ... numbers specify unpainted gaps. When all numbers have 
   been used, the pattern is re-started from the beginning. 
   An optional last argument sets the dash offset, which defaults to 0.
   Calling [method setLineDash] with no arguments resets the dash pattern
   to a solid line.

[call [arg objectName] [method setLineStyle] [arg "width"] [arg "args"]]
   Sets the width and dash pattern for subsequent line drawing.
   Line width and any elements in the dash pattern must be non-negative numbers.
   [emph "args"] is a series of numbers (not a tcl list) which define a 
   dash pattern. The 1st, 3rd ... numbers give units to paint, 
   the 2nd, 4th ... numbers specify unpainted gaps. When all numbers have 
   been used, the pattern is re-started from the beginning. 
   This method do not support offsetting the pattern, see [method setLineDash]
   for a more complete method.

[call [arg objectName] [method line] [arg "x1"] [arg "y1"] [arg "x2"] [arg "y2"]]
   Draws a line from [emph "x1,"] [emph "y1"] to [emph "x2,"] [emph "y2"]

[call [arg objectName] [method curve] [arg "x1"] [arg "y1"] [arg "x2"] [arg "y2"]\
   [arg "x3"] [arg "y3"] [opt [arg "x4 y4"]] ]
   If [emph "x4,"] [emph "y4"] are present, draws a cubic bezier from [emph "x1,"]
   [emph "y1"] to [emph "x4,"] [emph "y4"] with control points [emph "x2,"] [emph "y2"] and
   [emph "x3,"] [emph "y3"]. Otherwise draws a quadratic bezier from [emph "x1,"]
   [emph "y1"] to [emph "x3,"] [emph "y3"], with control point [emph "x2,"]
   [emph "y2"]

[call [arg objectName] [method polygon] [opt [arg "x y"]...] [opt [arg "option value"]...]]
   Draw a polygon. There must be at least 3 points.
   The polygon is closed back to the first coordinate unless [arg -closed] is false in which case a poly-line is drawn.
   [list_begin options]
      [opt_def -filled "[arg bool]   (default 0)"]
      Fill the polygon.
      [opt_def -stroke "[arg bool]   (default 1)"]
      Draw an outline of the polygon.
      [opt_def -closed "[arg bool]   (default 1)"]
      Close polygon.
   [list_end]

[call [arg objectName] [method circle] [arg "x"] [arg "y"] [arg "radius"] [opt [arg "option value"]...]]
   Draw a circle at the given center coordinates.
   [list_begin options]
      [opt_def -filled "[arg bool]   (default 0)"]
      Fill the circle.
      [opt_def -stroke "[arg bool]   (default 1)"]
      Draw an outline of the circle.
   [list_end]

[call [arg objectName] [method oval] [arg "x"] [arg "y"] [arg "radiusx"] [arg "radiusy"] [opt [arg "option value"]...]]
   Draw an oval at the given center coordinates.
   [list_begin options]
      [opt_def -filled "[arg bool]   (default 0)"]
      Fill the oval.
      [opt_def -stroke "[arg bool]   (default 1)"]
      Draw an outline of the oval.
   [list_end]

[call [arg objectName] [method arc] [arg "x"] [arg "y"] [arg "radiusx"] [arg "radiusy"] [arg "phi"] [arg "extend"] [opt [arg "option value"]...]]
   Draw an arc, following the given oval. The arc starts at angle [arg "phi"], given in degrees starting in the "east" direction, counting counter clockwise. The arc extends [arg "extend"] degrees.
   [list_begin options]
      [opt_def -filled "[arg bool]   (default 0)"]
      Fill the arc.
      [opt_def -stroke "[arg bool]   (default 1)"]
      Draw an outline of the arc.
      [opt_def -style "[arg arc|pieslice|chord]   (default [arg arc])"]
      Defines the style of the arc. An [arg "arc"] draws the perimeter of the arc and is never filled. A [arg "pieslice"] closes the arc with lines to the center of the oval. A [arg "chord"] closes the arc directly.
   [list_end]

[call [arg objectName] [method arrow] [arg "x1"] [arg "y1"] [arg "x2"] [arg "y2"] [arg "size"] [opt [arg "angle"]]]
   Draw an arrow. Default [arg "angle"] is 20 degrees.

[call [arg objectName] [method rectangle] [arg "x"] [arg "y"] [arg "width"] [arg "height"] [opt [arg "option value"]...]]
   Draw a rectangle.
   [list_begin options]
      [opt_def -filled "[arg bool]   (default 0)"]
      Fill the rectangle.
      [opt_def -stroke "[arg bool]   (default 1)"]
      Draw an outline of the rectangle.
   [list_end]


[call [arg objectName] [method roundedRect] [arg "x"] [arg "y"] [arg "width"] [arg "height"] [opt [arg "option value"]...]]
   Draw a rectangle with rounded corners (Bezier approximation).
   [list_begin options]
      [opt_def -radius "[arg value]   (default 5)"]
      Corner radius in the document's current unit. Automatically clamped
      to half the shorter side.
      [opt_def -filled "[arg bool]   (default 0)"]
      Fill the rectangle.
      [opt_def -stroke "[arg bool]   (default 1)"]
      Draw an outline of the rectangle.
   [list_end]

[call [arg objectName] [method clip] [arg "x"] [arg "y"] [arg "width"] [arg "height"]]
   Create a clip region. To cancel a clip region you must restore a graphic context that was saved before.

[call [arg objectName] [method gsave]]
   Save graphic/text context. (I.e. insert a raw PDF "q" command).
This saves the settings of at least these calls: [method clip], [method setBgColor], [method setFillColor], [method setStrokeColor], [method setAlpha], [method setLineStyle], [method setLineWidth], [method setLineDash], [method setFont], and [method setLineSpacing].
   Each call to [method gsave] should be followed by a later call to [method grestore] in the same page.

[call [arg objectName] [method grestore]]
   Restore graphic/text context. (I.e. insert a raw PDF "Q" command).
Restores all saved state including the raw-coordinate mode set by
[method translate], [method rotate], [method scale], or [method transform].
After [method grestore], drawing commands return to user-coordinate mode
(orient + margin active).

[call [arg objectName] [method transform] [arg a] [arg b] [arg c] [arg d] [arg e] [arg f]]
   Apply a PDF transformation matrix ([cmd cm] operator) to the current
graphics state. Use with [method gsave]/[method grestore].
[para]
After this call, drawing commands ([method line], [method rectangle] etc.)
work in raw-coordinate mode: y points upward, no margin, no orient flip.
Text commands ([method text]) use absolute [cmd Tm] positioning and are
[emph not] affected by transformation matrices.

[call [arg objectName] [method translate] [arg tx] [arg ty]]
   Shift the coordinate origin by [arg tx]/[arg ty] in the current unit,
respecting orient and margins. Equivalent to
[method transform] [const {1 0 0 1 tx ty}] (after coordinate conversion).
[para]
[emph {Raw-coordinate note:}] after [method translate], drawing commands
work in raw PDF space (y upward). A [method rectangle] [const {0 0 w h}]
draws [arg h] points [emph upward] from the new origin. To place the
bottom edge of a rectangle at user-y [arg y], pass
[const {[expr {$y + $h}]}] as the [arg ty] argument:
[example {
$pdf gsave
$pdf translate $x [expr {$y + $h}]  ;# bottom edge at user-y
$pdf rectangle 0 0 $w $h
$pdf grestore
}]

[call [arg objectName] [method rotate] [arg degrees]]
   Rotate the coordinate system clockwise by [arg degrees].
Typical use: [method translate] to the pivot point, then [method rotate].
Like [method translate], switches to raw-coordinate mode.

[call [arg objectName] [method scale] [arg sx] [arg sy]]
   Scale the coordinate system ([const 1.0] = unchanged).
Like [method translate], switches to raw-coordinate mode.

[call [arg objectName] [method getPageSize]]
   Return the full page dimensions as [const {{width height}}] in the
current unit (set via [option -unit] at creation time).
For A4 with [option {-unit mm}]: approximately [const {210.0 297.0}].
For A4 with [option {-unit p}]: approximately [const {595.0 842.0}]
(pdf4tcl rounds MediaBox to integer points).
Complements [method getDrawableArea] which excludes margins.

[call [arg objectName] [method getSubstCount]]
   Return how many characters could not be represented in the encoding of the
font in use and were replaced with [const ?] (or, in a subset without
[const ?], with [const .notdef]). The PDF stays valid; the text does not.
A non-zero value typically means Unicode text was drawn with a Latin-1 base
font -- use a CID font to avoid it. The count is per document and starts at
zero when the object is created.

[para]
Since 0.9.4.49 a CID font counts as well; before, a codepoint the font had no
glyph for was drawn as [const .notdef] and passed over, so the counter stayed
at zero for exactly the fonts used for text outside Latin-1. Each such
codepoint also appends to [var ::pdf4tcl::warnings], naming it and what was
drawn in its place, once per font and codepoint.

[para]
Exact from Tcl 9.0 on. Under Tcl 8.6 [cmd {encoding convertto}] performs its
own silent replacement and returns without an error, so pdf4tcl never learns
that a character was lost and the counter stays at zero. This is a property of
the Tcl release, not of pdf4tcl.

[call [arg objectName] [method addEmbeddedFile] [arg filename] [opt [arg options]]]
   Embed a file in the PDF.
When [option {-pdfa 3b}] is active the FileSpec OID is automatically
added to the document-level [const /AF] array in the Catalog
(ISO 19005-3 SS6.2.11.4).
[para]
Options: [option -contents], [option -mimetype], [option -description],
[option -afrelationship] (Alternative|Data|Source|Supplement|Unspecified).
[para]
[emph Note:] Embedded files are forbidden in PDF/A-1 (ISO 19005-1 SS6.1.7).

[call [arg objectName] [method addLayer] [arg name] [opt [option {-visible bool}]]]
   Add an Optional Content Group (OCG / layer) to the document.
Returns a layer ID for use with [method beginLayer].
[para]
[arg name] is the visible label shown in the viewer's layer panel.
[option -visible] controls default visibility (1 = shown, 0 = hidden, default: 1).
Use cases: debug grids ([option {-visible 0}]), letterhead variants,
watermarks ([option {-visible 0}]).
[para]
[emph Note:] [method addLayer] must be called before [method finish].
All layers are shared across all pages of the document.

[call [arg objectName] [method beginLayer] [arg layerId]]
   Open an Optional Content Group block. All drawing commands until
[method endLayer] belong to this layer.
[arg layerId] is the OID returned by [method addLayer].
Inserts a [cmd BDC] operator into the content stream.

[call [arg objectName] [method endLayer]]
   Close the current Optional Content Group block.
Inserts an [cmd EMC] operator into the content stream.

[example {
set lGrid [$pdf addLayer "Debug-Raster" -visible 0]
set lKopf [$pdf addLayer "Briefkopf"   -visible 1]

$pdf beginLayer $lGrid
  $pdf setStrokeColor 0.9 0.9 0.9
  for {set x 0} {$x <= 595} {incr x 50} { $pdf line $x 0 $x 842 }
$pdf endLayer

$pdf beginLayer $lKopf
  $pdf text "Musterfirma GmbH" -x 40 -y 28
$pdf endLayer
}]

[call [arg objectName] [method _WriteXrefTable] [arg idHash] [arg encdict_oid] [arg metadata_oid]]
[call [arg objectName] [method _WriteXrefStream] [arg idHash] [arg encdict_oid] [arg metadata_oid]]
   Internal methods for writing the cross-reference section.
[method _WriteXrefTable] writes the classic text-based xref table and trailer dict.
[method _WriteXrefStream] writes a binary XRef stream object (PDF 1.5+,
required for PDF/A-2b+). The XRef stream object contains all trailer entries
([const /Root], [const /Info], [const /ID]) eliminating the need for a
separate trailer dictionary.
[para]
Selection is automatic: [option {-pdfa 2b}] uses [method _WriteXrefStream],
all other modes use [method _WriteXrefTable].

[list_end]


[subsection {OBJECT CONFIGURATION}]

All pdf4tcl objects understand the options from [sectref {PAGE CONFIGURATION}],
which defines default page settings when used with a pdf4tcl object.
The objects also understand the following configuration options:

[list_begin options]

[opt_def -cmyk [arg boolean]]

If true, pdf4tcl will try to generate the document in CMYK color space.
See [cmd ::pdf4tcl::rgb2Cmyk] for a way to control color translation.
Default value is false.
This option can only be set at object creation.

[opt_def -compress [arg boolean]]

Pages will be zlib compressed if this option is set to true.
Default value is true.
This option can only be set at object creation.

[opt_def -file [arg filename]]

Continuously write pdf to [arg filename] instead of storing it
in memory.
This option can only be set at object creation.

[opt_def -unit [arg defaultunit]]

Defines default unit for coordinates and distances.  Any value given without
a unit is interpreted using this unit.  
See [sectref UNITS] for valid units.
Default value is "p" as in points.
This option can only be set at object creation.

[opt_def -pdfa [arg level]]

Enables PDF/A conformance for the document.
Valid values are [const ""] (none, default), [const 1b], [const 2b] and
[const 3b] for conformance level B, and [const 1a], [const 2a] and
[const 3a] for level A.

[para]
Level A adds tagged PDF, a natural language and Unicode mappings on top of
level B. pdf4tcl always writes the Unicode mappings; the other two are the
caller's job and are checked when the document is finished. Without tagging,
or without a language, writing the document raises an error rather than
putting [term "pdfaid:conformance A"] into a file that does not have what
that claims. In other words, [const 3a] needs at least:

[example {
$pdf tagged 1 -lang de-DE
}]

and content actually marked up with [method tagBegin] and friends. What
pdf4tcl cannot check is whether the markup is any good: a tree in which every
paragraph is [const /P] and every heading is [const /H1] satisfies this and
tells a reader nothing.

[para]
Note that every conformance level requires all font programs to be embedded,
which the 14 standard fonts cannot be. Using one of them while [option -pdfa]
is set produces a valid PDF that is not a valid PDF/A, and pdf4tcl notes this
once in [var ::pdf4tcl::warnings].
[para]
When [option -pdfa] is set, pdf4tcl automatically embeds:
[list_begin itemized]
[item] An XMP metadata stream with the pdfaid identification schema
([term pdfaid:part] and [term pdfaid:conformance]).
[item] An OutputIntent dictionary with an sRGB ICC profile ([const /GTS_PDFA1]).
[item] Suppresses [const {/Group /S /Transparency}] on all pages (required
for PDF/A-1).
[list_end]
[para]
Note: Standard Type\u00a01 fonts (Helvetica, Times, Courier) are not embedded.
For full PDF/A conformance, use CID fonts created with
[cmd ::pdf4tcl::createFontSpecCID].
[para]
Default value is [const ""].
This option can only be set at object creation.

[opt_def -pdfa-icc [arg path]]

Explicit path to the sRGB ICC profile file used for the OutputIntent when
[option -pdfa] is [const 1b] or [const 2b].
[para]
If not specified, pdf4tcl searches for the profile in standard system
locations (e.g. [file /usr/share/color/icc/ghostscript/srgb.icc]).
[para]
This option can only be set at object creation.

[opt_def -userpassword [arg string]]

Set a user (open) password for the document.
When set, the document cannot be opened without this password.
[para]
The encryption level is controlled by [option -encversion]:
AES-128 (default, V=4/R=4) or AES-256 (V=5/R=6).
[para]
Note: [option -userpassword] and [option -pdfa] cannot be combined --
PDF/A forbids encryption (ISO 19005).
[para]
This option can only be set at object creation.

[opt_def -ownerpassword [arg string]]

Set an owner password for the document.
The owner password grants full access regardless of user-password
restrictions.
When only [option -ownerpassword] is set (no [option -userpassword]),
the document opens without a password but is protected from modification.
[para]
This option can only be set at object creation.

[opt_def -encversion [arg integer]]

Set the encryption version. Must be [const 4] (AES-128, default) or
[const 5] (AES-256).
[para]
[const 4]: AES-128, V=4/R=4, PDF 1.5+. Pure Tcl, no external programs.
[para]
[const 5]: AES-256, V=5/R=6, PDF 2.0. SHA-384/SHA-512 is selected
automatically from three backends in priority order:
[const tcl-sha] (C extension, fastest, optional),
[cmd openssl] (if in PATH), or a pure-Tcl fallback (always available,
approx. 24 seconds per document). No external program is required.
[para]
This option can only be set at object creation.

[opt_def -permissions [arg value]]

Set the access rights for users of an encrypted document.
Only meaningful in combination with [option -userpassword].
Accepted values: [const all] (default, /P=-196), [const none],
[const readonly] (print only), a list of flags
([const print], [const hq-print], [const modify], [const copy],
[const annotate], [const fill-forms], [const accessibility],
[const assemble]), or a direct integer /P value.
[para]
Note: permissions are enforced by conforming viewers only.
The owner password always grants full access.
[para]
This option can only be set at object creation.

[list_end]

[subsection {PAGE CONFIGURATION}]

[list_begin options]

[opt_def -paper [arg name]]

The argument of this option defines the paper size.
The paper size may be a string like "a4", where valid values
are available through [cmd ::pdf4tcl::getPaperSizeList].
Supported series include ISO A (a0-a10), ISO B (b0-b10), ISO C (c0-c10),
and the oversize formats [const 4a0] and [const 2a0].
North American sizes ([const letter], [const legal], [const executive])
are also supported.
Paper size may also be a two element list specifying width and height.
[para]
The default value of this option is "a4".

[opt_def -landscape [arg boolean]]

If true, paper width and height are switched.
[para]
The default value of this option is false.

[opt_def -orient [arg boolean]]

This sets the orientation of the y axis of the coordinate system.
With [option -orient] false, origin is in the bottom left corner.
With [option -orient] true, origin is in the top left corner.
[para]
The default value of this option is true.

[opt_def -margin [arg values]]

The margin is a one, two or four element list of margins.
For one element, it specifies all margins.
Two elements specify left/right and top/bottom.
Four elements specify left, right, top and bottom.
[para]
The default value of this option is zero.

[opt_def -rotate [arg angle]]

This value defines a rotation angle for the display of the page.
Allowed values are multiples of 90.
[para]
The default value of this option is zero.

[list_end]

[section EXAMPLES]
[example_begin]
  pdf4tcl::new mypdf -paper a3
  mypdf startPage
  mypdf setFont 12 Courier
  mypdf text "Hejsan" -x 50 -y 50
  mypdf write -file mypdf.pdf
  mypdf destroy
[example_end]

[subsection {Unit conversion procs}]

The following procs convert common units to PDF points (1 pt = 1/72 inch).
They can be used directly in any coordinate or distance argument.

[list_begin definitions]
[def "[cmd pdf4tcl::mm] [arg value]"]
Convert millimetres to points.
[def "[cmd pdf4tcl::cm] [arg value]"]
Convert centimetres to points.
[def "[cmd pdf4tcl::in] [arg value]"]
Convert inches to points.
[def "[cmd pdf4tcl::pt] [arg value]"]
Identity conversion (returns the value as a floating-point number).
[list_end]

Example:
[example {
$pdf text "Hello" -x [pdf4tcl::mm 20] -y [pdf4tcl::mm 267]
$pdf roundedRect [pdf4tcl::mm 20] [pdf4tcl::mm 50]                  [pdf4tcl::mm 80] [pdf4tcl::mm 30]                  -radius [pdf4tcl::mm 5] -filled 1
}]


[section {PACKAGE VARIABLES}]

[var ::pdf4tcl::warnings]

A list of non-fatal warnings accumulated during PDF generation. Populated by

[list_begin itemized]
[item] [method setAlpha] when called with a value less than 1.0 under
[option {-pdfa 1b}] (transparency forbidden by ISO 19005-1 SS6.1.3);
[item] [method facturx] when the attached XML is not valid Factur-X/ZUGFeRD;
[item] the PDF string writer when a control character had to be dropped from a
string (see below). Codepoints above U+00FF are transliterated or replaced
silently and are [emph not] reported here -- typographic punctuation is
ordinary prose and would produce a warning for nearly every document.
[list_end]

The string writer adds at most one entry per kind and process, so a broken
document does not produce one line per string. Clearing the list with
[cmd {set ::pdf4tcl::warnings {}}] also re-arms that report.

Nothing is written to [const stderr]: where diagnostics appear is the caller's
decision.

[example {
set ::pdf4tcl::warnings {}
set pdf [pdf4tcl::new %AUTO% -pdfa 1b]
$pdf startPage
$pdf setAlpha 0.5      ;# appends warning -- no exception raised
$pdf endPage
$pdf destroy
foreach w $::pdf4tcl::warnings { puts "WARNING: $w" }
}]

Reset before each document with [cmd {set ::pdf4tcl::warnings {}}].
The PDF is generated regardless of any warnings in this list.

[var ::pdf4tcl::_md5Backend]

Set by [method _InitMD5Backend] on first use.
Values: [const tcllib], [const openssl], [const pure-tcl].
All three compute the same MD5; the choice is about availability and speed,
not strength. Read-only; for diagnostics only.

[var ::pdf4tcl::_randBackend]

Set on first use when a document is encrypted. Values: [const urandom]
(reads [file /dev/urandom]), [const twapi] ([cmd ::twapi::random_bytes]),
[const powershell] (RandomNumberGenerator via [cmd {exec powershell}]) or
[const none].

[para]
These bytes are the AES file key, the initialisation vectors and the salts,
so they must come from a cryptographic source. If none of the three is
available the encryption raises an error rather than falling back to
[cmd {expr rand()}], whose 31 bit state seeded from the clock would give an
AES-256 key at most 31 bits of entropy. A document that cannot be written is
better than one that only appears to be encrypted. Read-only; for diagnostics
only.

[section CHANGES]

[subsection {Version 0.9.4.42}]
[para]
[list_begin itemized]
[item] [cmd ::pdf4tcl::catPdf] folds objects with an identical body onto one
and renumbers densely. Documents built from the same template share their
embedded font programs; measured on two documents of 28961 bytes each, the
result went from 57757 to 33762 bytes. Pages, the catalog and structure
elements are never folded.
[item] [cmd ::pdf4tcl::catPdf] folds objects with an identical body onto one
and renumbers densely. Documents built from the same template share their
embedded font programs; measured on two documents of 28961 bytes each, the
result went from 57757 to 33762 bytes. Pages, the catalog and structure
elements are never folded.
[item] [option -markstyle] decides how the mark of a check box or radio
button is drawn: [const font] uses a ZapfDingbats glyph as before,
[const vector] draws it with lines and curves, and [const auto], the default,
picks vectors where the document claims PDF/UA or a level A conformance. The
glyph cannot be embedded and both standards require embedded font programs,
so a single check box used to make a document non-conformant whatever else it
did right. Documents claiming nothing keep the appearance they always had.
[item] [file tools/check-conformance.py]: new. Reads a document's XMP, works
out which profiles it claims and runs veraPDF against those; takes files or
directories. A document claiming nothing is reported as such rather than
failed against a profile it never promised.
[item] [file src/tagged.tcl] ([cmd TagAnnotEntries]): an annotation is
attached to an open [const Form] element as well, not only to [const Link]
and [const Annot]. ISO 32000-1 table 337 gives [const /Form] as the structure
type of an interactive field and PDF/UA clause 7.18.1 wants the widget
annotation attached through [const /OBJR]; [method tagBegin] accepted the
type all along, so the element sat in the tree while the field stayed
unreachable, and the warning about unattached annotations fired for a
document that had done everything right.
[list_end]

[subsection {Version 0.9.4.41}]
[para]
[list_begin itemized]
[item] [option -pdfa] accepts the conformance levels [const 1a],
[const 2a] and [const 3a]. Level A requires tagged PDF, a natural language
and Unicode mappings; pdf4tcl always wrote the mappings, and the other two
are checked when the document is finished. Missing either raises an error
rather than claiming a conformance the file does not have.
[item] Using one of the 14 standard fonts while [option -pdfa] is set is
reported in [var ::pdf4tcl::warnings]. Those fonts have no embeddable font
program, so the document cannot validate as PDF/A -- which used to happen
without a word. The file is still produced; it is a valid PDF, just not a
valid PDF/A.
[item] [file examples/tagged.tcl] and [file examples/facturx.tcl] are both
built as PDF/A-3a now, and the invoice is tagged. They demonstrate a document
that is archivable and accessible at the same time.
[item] [file src/cat.tcl] ([cmd WritePdf]): the binary comment in the file
header has four bytes above 127, as ISO 19005 clause 6.1.2 requires; it had
three, which readers accept but PDF/A validation does not. The header version
is taken from the input documents rather than a fixed 1.4.
[item] A document that is both PDF/A and PDF/UA now declares the
[const pdfuaid] namespace through a [const pdfaExtension] schema, sharing
one [const pdfaExtension:schemas] property with the Factur-X declaration
where both apply -- XMP allows that property once. PDF/A
clause 6.6.2.3.1 requires that for every XMP property outside the predefined
schemas; without it veraPDF rejected the file over [const pdfuaid:part], so
[option -pdfa] and [method tagged] with [option -ua] could not be combined at
all. The declaration is written only when both are set.
[list_end]

[subsection {Version 0.9.4.40}]
[para]
[list_begin itemized]
[item] [file src/cat.tcl] ([cmd MergeStructure]): [cmd ::pdf4tcl::catPdf]
merges the logical structure of tagged documents instead of dropping it. The
parent tree keys of the appended document are shifted so they stay unique,
in the tree and in every [const /StructParents] and [const /StructParent],
and the [const /Document] subtrees end up under one root. Chainable.
[item] MCIDs are deliberately not renumbered: they are scoped to one page's
content stream, and merging documents does not merge pages, so only the
parent tree keys have to be unique.
[item] Mixing tagged and untagged input warns rather than producing a half
tagged document, in both directions.
[item] Two older properties of the merge are now documented: the first
document's catalog is kept, so its title survives into the result, and
embedded fonts are not shared between the inputs.
[item] [file src/cat.tcl] ([cmd RenumberDict]): the reference mapping was not
passed on, so the redirection of the appended document's Pages object was
skipped for the trailer, root and info dictionaries. It never showed because
[cmd AppendPdf] rebuilds the Pages object afterwards.
[list_end]

[subsection {Version 0.9.4.39}]
[para]
[list_begin itemized]
[item] [file src/color.tcl]: new. [cmd GetColor], the color setters and the
RGB/CMYK conversions moved out of [file src/main.tcl] and
[file src/helpers.tcl]. [cmd ::pdf4tcl::rgb2Cmyk] and
[cmd ::pdf4tcl::cmyk2Rgb] keep their namespace and stay overridable.
[item] Color components are range checked. Values outside 0.0 to 1.0 used to
be written verbatim as operands of [const rg], [const RG], [const k] or
[const K], where ISO 32000-1 clause 8.6.4 requires that range. Readers clamp
them silently, so the document simply had the wrong color and no tool
complained. They now raise an error.
[item] The 147 standard color names are resolved from a built-in table
instead of [cmd {winfo rgb}], which needs Tk and a display. Color names now
work in a headless script. Names outside the table still ask Tk.
[item] Gradients use the same color pipeline as everything else. The separate
parser knew eight names plus hex, so [method linearGradient] accepted
strictly less than [method setFillColor] in the same document; CMYK lists and
names such as [const navy] were errors. The shading also declares
[const /DeviceCMYK] rather than always [const /DeviceRGB] when the document
is CMYK.
[item] Tagged PDF: an annotation created while no [const Link] or
[const Annot] element is open is reported in [var ::pdf4tcl::warnings]. Such
an annotation is not reachable from the structure tree -- the link still
works when clicked, but assistive technology never sees it, and PDF/UA
rule 7.18 cannot be met. Reported once per document.
[list_end]

[subsection {Version 0.9.4.38}]
[para]
[list_begin itemized]
[item] Demos and documentation. No change to the library API.
[item] [file demo]: the demo suite is complete again. All 35 scripts
are covered by [file run-all-demos.tcl], entries carry an optional reason for
being skipped, and [option --alle] runs those too. Five scripts that never
ran are fixed, and output goes to [file demo/out] instead of next to the
scripts.
[item] [file demo/demo-tagged.tcl]: new, demonstrates Tagged PDF.
[item] [file demo/demo-unicode-tabelle.tcl]: warns that the blocks
above U+FFFF cannot be rendered under Tcl 8, detected at runtime rather than
from the version number.
[item] [file src/main.tcl] ([cmd CanvasDoTkoPathItem]): the item id was
overwritten with the PDF image id before being used to ask the widget for
coordinates. Coordinates are determined first now.
[item] [file src/main.tcl]: both window branches delete the photo created by
[cmd {image create photo -format window}] after reading the pixels.
[item] [file doc/en]: 27 of the 93 documented methods appeared in no
guide. New documents for images, canvas export and annotations, plus a
README index; bookmarks, XObjects, the text cursor, arcs, clipping and the
state queries added to the existing ones.
[list_end]

[subsection {Version 0.9.4.37}]
[para]
[list_begin itemized]
[item] Tagged PDF: annotations can be attached to the structure tree.
[method tagBegin] accepts [const Link] and [const Annot]; an annotation
created while such an element is open receives a [const /StructParent] key
and the element an [const /OBJR] entry. Link annotations also get
[const /Contents] from the element's [option -alt] when they have none, as
PDF/UA requires.
[item] [file src/main.tcl] ([cmd AddAnnot]): new. All eight places that
created annotation objects now go through it, so the tagging module sees
every annotation.
[item] Tagged PDF: new attributes [option -listnumbering] on [const L] and
[option -id] and [option -headers] on [const TH] and [const TD].
[item] Tagged PDF: a page with annotations is now written with
[const {/Tabs /S}] instead of [const {/Tabs /R}] while tagging is enabled
(ISO 14289-1 clause 7.18.3). Untagged documents are unaffected.
[item] [file tools/check-tagged.py]: checks that every annotation is claimed
by exactly one [const /OBJR], that its [const /StructParent] resolves back to
that element, and that link annotations carry [const /Contents].
[list_end]

[subsection {Version 0.9.4.36}]
[para]
[list_begin itemized]
[item] [file src/tagged.tcl]: new. Tagged PDF, the logical structure of
ISO 32000-1 clause 14.7 and 14.8. New methods [method tagged],
[method tagBegin], [method tagEnd], [method tagText], [method tagArtifact]
and [method tagArtifactEnd]. Writes [const /StructTreeRoot] with a parent
tree, [const /MarkInfo], [const /StructParents] per page, and [const /Alt],
[const /ActualText], [const /Lang], [const /T] and [const /Scope] on the
elements. Off by default.
[item] [file src/main.tcl] ([cmd _BuildXMPStream]): writes the PDF/UA
identification schema [const pdfuaid:part] when [method tagged] was called
with [option -ua]. Opt-in, because the entry asserts conformance that pdf4tcl
cannot verify.
[item] [file src/cat.tcl] ([cmd StripStructure]): [cmd ::pdf4tcl::catPdf]
now removes the logical structure of tagged inputs and notes this in
[var ::pdf4tcl::warnings]. Previously the merged file claimed to be tagged
while the second document's pages indexed the first document's parent tree:
two pages both carrying [const {/StructParents 0}], a tree holding only the
first document's heading, and no error raised. A file that misreports its
structure is worse than one without any, because a reader trusts
[const /MarkInfo] and follows the wrong tree instead of the paint order.
[item] [file src/cat.tcl] ([cmd WritePdf]): fixed a typo where the final
[cmd WriteCh] passed [const po] instead of [const pos], updating the position
counter in a variable nobody reads.
[item] [file tests/tagged.test]: new, 37 tests.
[item] [file tools/check-tagged.py]: new. Walks the structure tree,
reconstructs the text each element actually paints, and checks marked content
balance, MCID numbering and nesting, [const /StructParents] uniqueness across
pages, parent tree coverage and orphaned sequences.
[item] [file examples/tagged.tcl]: new. Headings, list, table with
[const /Scope], figure with [const /Alt], artifacts and a paragraph spanning
a page break. Validates with veraPDF 1.28.2 as PDF/UA-1, 106 rules and 1357
checks passed, none failed.
[list_end]

